ring all squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\).  \n   – These \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, cover every square except the diagonal holes, and respect the condition that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square.  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   – The lower bound \\(n\\) and the explicit construction with \\(n\\) tiles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).  \n\n5. **Why the case “\\(1=0\\)” fails.**  \n   – The problem demands **exactly one** uncovered unit square per row and per column.  Setting the number of uncovered squares to zero contradicts this requirement, so such a configuration does not satisfy the constraints.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  A rectangular tile is an axis‑aligned sub‑rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it covers a set of unit squares that is a Cartesian product of a set of rows and a set of columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each of the \\(n\\) rows must contain precisely one uncovered unit square, there are exactly \\(n\\) uncovered squares in total.  The same holds for the columns, hence the uncovered squares must occupy distinct rows and distinct columns.  Consequently they form a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nRenaming rows and columns (which does not affect the number of tiles needed) we may assume \\(\\pi\\) is the identity permutation.  Thus the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij}),\\qquad a_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if the square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered},\\\\\n0 & \\text{if }(i,j)\\in U.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWith the diagonal holes we have  \n\n\\[\nA = J - I,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n*Rank of \\(A\\).*  \nThe eigenvalues of \\(J\\) are \\(n\\) (once) and \\(0\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  Hence the eigenvalues of \\(J-I\\) are \\(n-1\\) (once) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  None of them is zero, so \\(J-I\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n.\n\\]\n\n*Relation to tiles.*  \nA tile covering rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) corresponds to the rank‑1 matrix  \n\n\\[\nM(R,C)=\\mathbf{1}_R\\mathbf{1}_C^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{1}_R\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_C\\)) is the column (resp. row) indicator vector of \\(R\\) (resp. \\(C\\)).  Its support is exactly the set \\(R\\times C\\) of squares covered by that tile.\n\nBecause the tiles are required to be **disjoint**, the matrices \\(M(R_k,C_k)\\) of the individual tiles form a *partition* of the support of \\(A\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{t} M(R_k,C_k),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t\\) is the number of tiles.  The rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks, and each \\(M(R_k,C_k)\\) has rank \\(1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}\\bigl(M(R_k,C_k)\\bigr)=t.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\), we obtain the **lower bound**\n\n\\[\nt\\ge n.\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2025\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nTake the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\) as in step 1.  For each row \\(i\\) place a single tile\n\n\\[\nR_i = \\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering all squares of row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*   The tiles are pairwise disjoint beca