ssible. For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nA placement of tiles is *admissible* if  \n\n* every tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines,\n* no two tiles overlap,\n* each unit square of the \\(n\\times n\\) board is covered by at most one tile, and\n* in every row and in every column there is **exactly one** unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of uncovered unit squares.  \nBecause each row contains precisely one uncovered square, \\(|U|=n\\).  \nSince each column also contains precisely one uncovered square, the projection of \\(U\\) onto the set of columns is a bijection.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered cells are the entries of a permutation matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \nThe uncovered cells are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe cells left of, respectively right of, the diagonal.\n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid c+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_c\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\).  \nAll cells of \\(L\\) belong to exactly one of the sets \\(V_c\\); the sets are pairwise disjoint.  \nHence the family \\(\\{V_c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) tiles \\(L\\) with \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nW_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i\\le c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(W_c\\