ma(i) = pi(i). Then the uncovered squares become (sigma(i), pi(i)) = (pi(i), pi(i)). So they map row i to pi(i). But the rectangles also get rows permuted accordingly. Does that preserve the rectangle shape? Let's examine: Suppose we have a rectangle covering rows i..j and columns a..b. After applying sigma to rows, the rectangle covers rows sigma(i), sigma(i+1), ..., sigma(j). Since sigma is a permutation, the set {sigma(i), sigma(i+1), ..., sigma(j)} may not be a consecutive interval. So the shape is not a rectangle. So the reduction is flawed.\n\nBut perhaps they intend to apply the same permutation to both rows and columns? They only permute rows, leaving columns unchanged. However, they could also permute columns accordingly to maintain rectangle shape. But they didn't. So the reduction may be invalid. However, the lower bound argument they give is based on the diagonal case. If the reduction is invalid, the lower bound may not hold for arbitrary permutation. But perhaps the lower bound can be proven directly without reduction. Let's examine the lower bound argument: They consider left half L = { (i,j) | j < i } and for each column c (1 <= c <= n-1) the cell p_c = (c+1, c) is the topmost cell in column c within L. They claim no rectangle covering p_c can also contain any cell from a different column c' != c, because that would include a diagonal cell (c+1, c+1). This argument uses the fact that the uncovered squares are on the diagonal (i,i). If the uncovered squares are not on the diagonal but some permutation, the argument fails. However, they claim we can reduce to diagonal case via row permutation. But as argued, that reduction may be invalid. So the lower bound may not be valid for arbitrary permutation. But perhaps the lower bound can be proven for any permutation directly, not requiring diagonal case. Let's examine: For any permutation pi, consider the uncovered squares (i, pi(i)). For each column c, the uncovered square in column c is at row r = pi^{-1}