ms a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,f(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by pairwise disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq f(i)\\}= [n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\n\\]\n\nthe set of squares that have to be tiled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the identity permutation  \n\nPermuting the columns of the board does not change the geometric nature of a rectangle: a rectangle stays a rectangle after a column permutation.  Hence the minimal number of tiles is invariant under a simultaneous permutation of the columns.  Applying the column permutation that sends column \\(f(i)\\) to column \\(i\\) for every \\(i\\) transforms the uncovered set \\(U\\) into the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently it suffices to treat the case  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom now on we assume (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the part of row \\(i\\) that lies strictly to the right of the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\).  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the part of column \\(j\\) that lies strictly below the diagonal square \\((j,j)\\).\n\n*Properties.*  \n\n*   Each \\(R_i\\) and each \\(C_j\\) is a genuine rectangle (its row and column sets are intervals).  \n*   No rectangle contains a diagonal square, because the column set of \\(R_i\\) consists of columns \\(>i\\) and the row set of \\(C_j\\) consists of rows \\(>j\\).  \n*   The families \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{C_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint: a cell \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x\\neq y\\) belongs either to a unique \\(R_x\\) (if \\(x<y\\)) or to a unique \\(C_y\\) (if \\(x>y\\)), never to both.  \n\nHence the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{C_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) form a partition of the set \\(A\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\tau(n)\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nLet a tiling of \\(A\\) consist of \\(k\\) rectangles.  For a rectangle \\(T\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(T)=\\min\\{j\\mid\\exists i\\;(i,j)\\in T\\},\\qquad\nr(T)=\\max\\{j\\mid\\exists i\\;(i,j)\\in T\\}\n\\]\n\nits leftmost and rightmost column, respectively.  Both numbers are well defined because a rectangle contains at least one column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor each column index \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) there exists a rectangle \\(T\\) in the tiling such that \\(r(T)=c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and consider the set  \n\n\\[\nL_c=\\{\\,i\\mid f(i)>c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(i\\in L_c\\) the cell \\((i,c)\\) lies to the left of the uncovered square \\((i,f(i))\\) and therefore belongs to \\(A\\); consequently it must be covered by some rectangle.  \nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangle covering \\((i,c)\\).  By definition of a rectangle, all columns of \\(T\\) form a contiguous interval containing \\(c\\).  Because \\(f(i)>c\\) and the diagonal square \\((i,f(i))\\) is forbidden, the column interval of \\(T\\) cannot extend beyond column \\(c\\); otherwise it would contain the forbidden column \\(f(i)\\).  Hence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) equals \\(c\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2  \nFor each column index \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) there exists a rectangle \\(T\\) in the tiling such that \\(\\ell(T)=c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and consider  \n\n\\[\nR_c=\\{\\,i\\mid f(i)<c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(i\\in R_c\\) the cell \\((i,c)\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square \\((i,f(i))\\) and thus belongs to \\(A\\).  Let \\(T\\) be a rectangle covering \\((i,c)\\).  Its column interval contains \\(c\\) and cannot contain the forbidden column \\(f(i)<c\\); therefore the interval cannot start left of \\(c\\).  Consequently the leftmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(c\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 3  \nThe rectangles supplied by Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 are all distinct.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA rectangle has a unique leftmost column and a unique rightmost column, and the leftmost column is never larger than the rightmost column.  Lemma\u202f1 produces rectangles whose rightmost column belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); Lemma\u202f2 produces rectangles whose leftmost column belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  The two sets of column indices are disjoint, so a single rectangle cannot satisfy both requirements.  Hence the two families are disjoint. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Conclusion of the lower bound  \n\nLemma\u202f1 yields at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles, Lemma\u202f2 yields another \\(n-1\\) rectangles, and Lemma\u202f3 guarantees that these \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are all different.  Therefore any tiling of \\(A\\) uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  Combining (4) and (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\tau(n)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. The case \\(n=2025\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(n=2025\\) into (6) gives\n\n\\[\n\\tau(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nThus the smallest possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Achievable intermediate numbers of tiles  \n\nThe construction in Section\u202f2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Starting from that tiling we may repeatedly **split** a tile along a grid line into two smaller rectangles; the split does not introduce any uncovered square and the resulting pieces are still valid tiles.  Each split raises the total number of tiles by exactly one.  \n\nBy performing a suitable number of splits we can obtain any integer number of tiles between the minimum \\(2n-2\\) and the trivial tiling consisting solely of \\(1\\times1\\) tiles, which uses \\(n^{2}-n\\) tiles.  Consequently the set of achievable numbers of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\{\\,t\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid 2n-2\\le t\\le n^{2}-n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor the concrete grid \\(n=2025\\) this set is\n\n\\[\n\\{\\,t\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid 4048\\le t\\le 2025^{2}-2025=4\\,098\\,600\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence **every integer between 4048 and\u202f4\u202f098\u202f600 inclusive can be realised**.\n\n--- \n\n**The problem is completely solved.**"
