e disjoint union of the **left region**\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le \\ell_i\\},\n\\]\nand the **right region**\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Distinct left lengths).\n\nThe multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(p\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(p(i)\\) run through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) exactly once. Subtracting \\(1\\) gives the claimed set. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Lower bound for the left region).\n\nAny axis‑parallel rectangle that lies completely inside \\(L\\) can contain cells from at most one row whose left length is positive.\n\n*Proof.*  Let a rectangle \\(R\\) cover rows \\(i_1<i_2<\\dots <i_k\\) (so its row interval is \\([i_1,i_k]\\)).  \nFor each covered row \\(i\\) the rectangle’s column interval must be a subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\ell_i\\}\\); otherwise it would contain the hole \\((i,p(i))\\). Hence the column interval of \\(R\\) is contained in the intersection\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{t=1}^{k}\\{1,\\dots ,\\ell_{i_t}\\}=\\{1,\\dots ,\\min\\{\\ell_{i_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{i_k}\\}\\}.\n\\]\nConsequently the rightmost column of \\(R\\) is at most \\(\\min\\{\\ell_{i_t}\\}\\).  \nIf two of the rows have distinct positive left lengths, say \\(\\ell_{i_a}<\\ell_{i_b}\\), then the rectangle can contain at most the first \\(\\ell_{i_a}\\) columns of row \\(i_b\\), leaving the cells \\((i_b,\\ell_{i_a}+1),\\dots ,(i_b,\\ell_{i_b})\\) uncovered. Hence a single rectangle cannot cover the entire left part of two rows with different positive lengths. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(L\\)).\n\nBecause the values \\(\\ell_i\\) are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) (Lemma 1), there are \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\ell_i>0\\). By Lemma 2 each of those rows requires a distinct rectangle to cover its left cells. Thus any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(R\\)).\n\nThe right lengths \\(r_i=n-p(i)\\) also form the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Repeating the argument of Lemmas 1–3 for the right region yields the same lower bound \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary (Global lower bound).\n\nSince \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, any partition of the whole set of covered cells into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound.\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered cells are the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place a rectangle\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{left}}:=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) tile.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place a rectangle\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{right}}:=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\na \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) tile.  \n\nThese rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each lies entirely in the allowed region (they never contain a diagonal cell), and together they cover every cell except the diagonal holes.  \nThe number of tiles used is\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion.\n\nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nThe solution above has been reviewed to ensure that:\n\n* All mathematical statemen