                "content": "answer.\n\n**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. Observe that the uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \n2. A rectangular tile cannot contain a diagonal (uncovered) square, therefore for any tile the set of its row indices and the set of its column indices are disjoint intervals. Consequently each tile lies entirely **above** the main diagonal (all its rows have smaller indices than all its columns) or entirely **below** it.  \n3. The board splits into the strict upper‑triangular part \\(U=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\) and the strict lower‑triangular part \\(L=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\). Tiles covering \\(U\\) are of the “upper’’ type, those covering \\(L\\) are of the “lower’’ type; the two families are independent.  \n4. **Upper bound:** For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) (here \\(n=2025\\)) place the rectangle  \n   \\[\n   R_k=[1,k]\\times[k+1,n],\n   \\]  \n   which covers all cells of \\(U\\) with column index at least \\(k+1\\). The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_k\\}\\) partition \\(U\\). Similarly the rectangles  \n   \\[\n   S_k=[k+1,n]\\times[1,k]\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\]  \n   partition \\(L\\). Hence \\(2(n-1)=4048\\) tiles suffice.  \n5. **Lower bound:**  \n   - In the upper part consider the set  \n     \\[\n     A=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n     \\]  \n     Any upper‑type rectangle is of the form \\([a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) with \\(b<c\\). If it contained two distinct points \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\), then its row interval would contain the index \\(i+1\\) while its column interval would also contain \\(i+1\\), forcing the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) to be inside the rectangle – impossible. Thus each upper‑type rectangle can contain **at most one** element of \\(A\\). Consequently at least \\(|A|=n-1\\)