er‑type rectangles are necessary.  \n   - An analogous argument with the set  \n     \\[\n     B=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n     \\]  \n     shows that at least \\(n-1\\) lower‑type rectangles are required.  \n   - Therefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.  \n\n6. Combining the upper and lower bounds yields the exact minimum \\(2(n-1)=4048\\) for \\(n=2025\\).\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet the board consist of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) where \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one square uncovered in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a **permutation matrix**:\n\\[\nU_0=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nfor some permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other squares, i.e. the set\n\\[\nE=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\]\nmust be covered by axis‑aligned rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines and which do not overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Geometry of a feasible rectangle  \n\nA rectangle is determined by a non‑empty interval of rows\n\\(I=[a,b]\\) and a non‑empty interval of columns \\(J=[c,d]\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nThe rectangle contains exactly the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in I,\\;j\\in J\\).\n\nIf a rectangle contained a cell \\((k,k)\\) of the permutation matrix,\nthat cell would be uncovered, contradicting the requirement that a tile\ncovers only uncovered squares.  Consequently a feasible rectangle must satisfy\n\n\\[\nI\\cap J=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of integers, (1) forces one of the\ntwo possibilities\n\n\\[\n\\max I < \\min J \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad \\max J < \\min I .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus every feasible rectangle lies **entirely above** the main diagonal\n(\\(\\max I<\\min J\\)) or **entirely below** it (\\(\\max J<\\min I\\)).  \nNo rectangle can contain cells from both sides of the diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Decomposition into upper and lower parts  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\},\\qquad\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(E\\) of cells to be tiled is the disjoint union \\(U\\cup L\\).\nRectangles of the first type in (2) cover only cells of \\(U\\);\nrectangles of the second type cover only cells of \\(L\\).\nHence the problem separates into two independent sub‑problems:\npartition \\(U\\) by “upper’’ rectangles and partition \\(L\\) by “lower’’ rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  An explicit tiling with \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_k=[1,k]\\times[k+1,n] .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\max[1,k]=k<k+1=\\min[k+1,n]\\), each \\(R_k\\) is an upper‑type rectangle.\nThe family \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions \\(U\\):\nevery cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to the unique rectangle\n\\(R_i\\) (since \\(i\\le i\\) and \\(j\\ge i+1\\)).  \nThus \\(U\\) can be tiled with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nSimilarly, for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_k=[k+1,n]\\times[1,k] .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(\\max[1,k]=k<k+1=\\min[k+1,n]\\) after swapping the roles of rows and columns,\nso each \\(S_k\\) is a lower‑type rectangle.\nThe family \\(\\{S_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions \\(L\\) analogously.\nConsequently the whole board can be tiled with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound: at least \\(n-1\\) upper‑type rectangles  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq U .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be any upper‑type rectangle, i.e. \\(b<c\\).\nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct points of \\(A\\), say\n\\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).\nBecause the row interval of \\(R\\) is contiguous, it must contain every integer\nbetween \\(i\\) and \\(j\\); in particular it contains \\(i+1\\).\nSimilarly the column interval, being contiguous, contains every integer\nbetween \\(i+1\\) and \\(j+1\\); thus it also contains \\(i+1\\).\nHence the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting (1).\nTherefore **no upper‑type rectangle can contain more than one element of \\(A\\)**.\n\nSince the rectangles covering \\(U\\) are pairwise disjoint, each must\ncontain a distinct element of \\(A\\).  Consequently at least \\(|A|=n-1\\)\nupper‑type rectangles are necessary to partition \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound: at least \\(n-1\\) lower‑type rectangles  \n\nApply the same argument to the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq L .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAny lower‑type rectangle (\\(\\max J<\\min I\\)) cannot contain two distinct\npoints of \\(B\\); otherwise it would also contain a diagonal cell.\nThus at least \\(|B|=n-1\\) lower‑type rectangles are required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom Sections 3–5 we have\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of rectangles } \\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1),\n\\]\nand the construction of Section 3 shows that exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles\nare sufficient.  Hence the minimum equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.  \n\n∎"
