cdot2-2\\).\n\n   The induction proves that any tiling requires at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board.\n\nSince the construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, this number is optimal. For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe have an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  \nA *hole* is a unit square that is left uncovered; the condition of the problem is that each row and each column contains exactly one hole.  \nThus the set of holes is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the holes are precisely the squares \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound \u2013 a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(i>1\\) the set \\(L_i\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\);  \nif \\(i<n\\) the set \\(R_i\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).  \n\nPlace a tile on every non\u2011empty set \\(L_i\\) and on every non\u2011empty set \\(R_i\\).  \nThe tiles are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows, and in each row the two tiles are separated by the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).  \nAll squares except the diagonal are covered, and the number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}\n=2n-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(4048\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound \u2013 at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n\nWe prove the statement for every integer \\(n\\ge2\\) by induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma (reordering)\n\nIf a