                "content": ".\n\n**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum possible number of tiles is exactly the side length of the grid, i.e. 2025.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. By permuting rows and columns we may assume the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \n2. A tile is a rectangle determined by a set of rows \\(L\\) and a set of columns \\(R\\) with \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).  \n3. Introduce two binary matrices \\(A,B\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times k}\\) (\\(n=2025\\), \\(k\\) = number of tiles) where  \n   \\[\n   A_{i t}=1\\iff i\\in L_t,\\qquad   \n   B_{i t}=1\\iff i\\in R_t .\n   \\]\n   The condition that every off‑diagonal square \\((i,j)\\) is covered by exactly one tile is equivalent to  \n   \\[\n   AB^{\\!\\top}=J-I,\n   \\]\n   where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity.  \n4. Over the reals, \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n\\). Since \\(\\operatorname{rank}(AB^{\\!\\top})\\le\\min\\{\\operatorname{rank}(A),\\operatorname{rank}(B)\\}\\le k\\), we obtain the lower bound \\(k\\ge n\\).  \n5. A construction with \\(k=n\\) tiles is given by the “row‑singleton” tiling: for each \\(i\\) take the rectangle  \n   \\[\n   L_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad R_i=[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}.\n   \\]\n   These \\(n\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, cover all off‑diagonal squares exactly once, and leave precisely the diagonal squares uncovered.  \n\nHence the minimum number of tiles equals \\(n=2025\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe grid consists of the set of unit squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nPermuting the columns by the permutation \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) does not change the number of tiles needed, and it moves the uncovered squares to the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nHence we may (and shall) assume that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Formalisation of a tile\n\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; therefore it is completely described by a set of rows \\(L\\subseteq [n]\\) and a set of columns \\(R\\subseteq [n]\\) with the property\n\n\\[\nL\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe tile covers precisely the squares\n\n\\[\nL\\times R=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in L,\\;j\\in R\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Incidence matrices\n\nSuppose that a tiling uses \\(k\\) tiles, which we index by \\(t=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \nDefine two binary matrices \\(A,B\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\,n\\times k}\\) by\n\n\\[\nA_{i t}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1,&\\text{if }i\\in L_t,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nB_{i t}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1,&\\text{if }i\\in R_t,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCondition (1) is equivalent to the entrywise product \\(A\\circ B=0\\); i.e. for every \\(i,t\\),\n\n\\[\nA_{i t}\\,B_{i t}=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) the number of tiles covering \\((i,j)\\) equals\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=1}^{k} A_{i t}B_{j t}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the tiling must cover each off‑diagonal square exactly once, (5) must equal 1 for all \\(i\\neq j\\) and must equal 0 for \\(i=j\\).  In matrix form this reads\n\n\\[\nAB^{\\!\\top}=J-I,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(J\\) is the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   A lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nTake the rank over the real numbers.  \nThe matrix \\(J-I\\) has eigenvalues \\(n-1\\) (with eigenvector \\((1,\\dots ,1)^{\\!T}\\)) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(\\operatorname{rank}(AB^{\\!\\top})\\le\\min\\{\\operatorname{rank}(A),\\operatorname{rank}(B)\\}\\le k\\).  Combining with (7) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling uses at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFor each row index \\(i\\in[n]\\) define the rectangle\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad R_i=[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L_i\\cap R_i=\\varnothing\\), each (9) is a legal tile.  \nThe collection \\(\\{L_i\\times R_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles: a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs to exactly the rectangle indexed by its row \\(i\\); the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) belong to none of the rectangles.  Consequently every off‑diagonal square is covered once and the diagonal squares remain uncovered, i.e. each row and each column has precisely one uncovered unit square.\n\nHence a tiling with \\(n\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Minimality\n\nThe lower bound (8) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles, while the construction in §4 achieves exactly \\(n\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\), the minimum number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
