onfined to the bottom row. Removing this rectangle leaves a diagram with \\(m-1\\) distinct positive row lengths, to which the induction hypothesis applies. Hence at least \\(m\\) rectangles are needed.\n\n   Applying the lemma to the left half‑board gives \\(\\#\\text{left‑tiles}\\ge n-1\\); applying it to the right half‑board gives \\(\\#\\text{right‑tiles}\\ge n-1\\).\n\n5. **Lower bound.**  \n   From step 2,\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{left‑tiles}+\\#\\text{right‑tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal.  \n   For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define two rectangles\n   \\[\n   A_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k<j\\le n\\},\n   \\qquad\n   B_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k<i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n   \\]\n   - \\(A_k\\) occupies rows \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) and columns \\(k+1\\) to \\(n\\) (above the diagonal).  \n   - \\(B_k\\) occupies rows \\(k+1\\) to \\(n\\) and columns \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) (below the diagonal).  \n\n   The families \\(\\{A_k\\}\\) and \\(\\{B_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every non‑diagonal cell exactly once. Hence we obtain a tiling with \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nNumber the rows \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, we may denote by \\(\\pi(i)\\) the column of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nThe condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square forces \\(\\pi\\) to be a bijection \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles, with interiors pairwise disjoint.\n\n#### 2.  A tile cannot straddle a hole in any row  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be a tile.  Write its row interval as \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and its column interval as \\([c_1,c_2]\\) (both inclusive).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) the tile occupies precisely the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid c_1\\le j\\le c_2\\}\\).\n\nAssume there exist rows \\(i,i'\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) with\n\\[\nc_2<\\pi(i)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad c_1>\\pi(i').\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThen \\(\\pi(i)\\) lies to the right of the tile in row \\(i\\) while \\(\\pi(i')\\) lies to the left of the tile in row \\(i'\\).  \nSince \\(\\pi(i)\\) and \\(\\pi(i')\\) are integers and \\(c_1\\le c_2\\), we have\n\\[\nc_1\\le c_2<\\pi(i)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\pi(i')<c_1.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\pi(i')<c_1\\le c_2<\\pi(i)\\), so the interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) contains an integer \\(c\\) with \\(\\pi(i')<c<\\pi(i)\\).  \nConsider the row \\(r\\) for which \\(\\pi(r)=c\\) (such a row exists because \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation).  \nSince \\(r\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) (the rows of the tile form a contiguous interval and contain both \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\)), the cell \\((r,c)\\) lies inside \\(T\\).  \nBut \\((r,c)\\) is an uncovered square, contradicting the definition of a tile.  \n\nThus (2) is impossible.  Hence for **every** row \\(i\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) we have either\n\\[\nc_2<\\pi(i)\\quad\\text{(the whole tile lies left of the hole in that row)},\n\\tag{3a}\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nc_1>\\pi(i)\\quad\\text{(the whole tile lies right of the hole in that row)}.\n\\tag{3b}\n\\]\n\nIf (3a) holds for one row and (3b) holds for another, the two corresponding inequalities would give a contradiction exactly as above.  Therefore **either (3a) holds for all rows of the tile or (3b) holds for all rows of the tile**.\n\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nEquation (3) shows that each tile is contained entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).  Consequently\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }L+\\#\\text{tiles covering }R.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  The left half‑board \\(L\\)  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) let\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=\\pi(i)-1,\n\\]\nthe number of cells of \\(L\\) in that row.  Because \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); in particular the \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThus exactly one row has \\(\\ell_i=0\\) (the row where \\(\\pi(i)=1\\)), and the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows have the distinct positive lengths\n\\[\n1,2,\\dots ,n-1.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  Lemma: covering a diagram with distinct row lengths  \n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let a left‑justified diagram consist of rows whose positive lengths are distinct, say \\(\\lambda_1<\\lambda_2<\\dots <\\lambda_m\\).  Any partition of this diagram into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least \\(m\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  We argue by induction on \\(m\\).  \nFor \\(m=1\\) the diagram itself is a rectangle, so the claim holds.\n\nAssume the statement true for diagrams with \\(m-1\\) rows of distinct positive length.  \nConsider a diagram with \\(m\\) rows of lengths \\(\\lambda_1<\\dots <\\lambda_m\\).  \nThe rightmost column of the diagram is column \\(\\lambda_m\\); by the strict increase it occurs **only** in the bottom row (the row of length \\(\\lambda_m\\)).  \nAny rectangle covering a cell of that column cannot extend above the bottom row, otherwise it would contain a non‑existent cell of that column in a higher row.  Hence such a rectangle is confined to the bottom row.  \nRemoving this rectangle eliminates the bottom row and possibly some cells of shorter rows, leaving a diagram whose set of positive row lengths is a subset of \\(\\{\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_{m-1}\\}\\) and still consists of distinct numbers.  By the induction hypothesis at least \\(m-1\\) rectangles are needed for the remainder, so the original diagram needs at least \\(1+(m-1)=m\\) rectangles. ∎\n\nApplying the lemma to the left half‑board \\(L\\) (whose positive row lengths are exactly the numbers in (6)) yields\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.  The right half‑board \\(R\\)  \n\nDefine the right lengths\n\\[\nr_i:=n-\\pi(i),\n\\]\nthe number of cells of \\(R\\) in row \\(i\\).  As before the multiset \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus the positive lengths of the rows of \\(R\\) are again the distinct numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nApplying the same lemma to \\(R\\) gives\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }R\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 6.  Lower bound for the whole board  \n\nFrom (5), (7) and (8) we obtain\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives the universal lower bound\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n#### 7.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  The uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\\[\nA_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k<j\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nB_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k<i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- **Shape.**  \\(A_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) lying entirely above the diagonal; \\(B_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\) lying entirely below the diagonal.  \n\n- **Disjointness.**  \n  *Rows.*  For fixed \\(k\\), \\(A_k\\) uses rows \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) while \\(B_k\\) uses rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\); thus \\(A_k\\cap B_k=\\varnothing\\).  \n  *Different \\(k\\).*  If \\(k<\\ell\\), then the column interval of \\(A_k\\) is \\((k,n]\\) while that of \\(A_\\ell\\) is \\((\\ell,n]\\); the two intervals are disjoint, and similarly the row intervals of \\(B_k\\) and \\(B_\\ell\\) are disjoint.  Hence all rectangles \\(\\{A_k\\}\\cup\\{B_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \n\n- **Coverage.**  \n  *Above the diagonal.*  Let \\((i,j)\\) satisfy \\(i<j\\).  Choose \\(k=i\\).  Then \\((i,j)\\in A_i\\) because \\(1\\le i\\le i\\) and \\(i<j\\le n\\).  \n  *Below the diagonal.*  Let \\((i,j)\\) satisfy \\(i>j\\).  Choose \\(k=j\\).  Then \\((i,j)\\in B_j\\) because \\(j<i\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le j\\).  \n  *Diagonal.*  The cells \\((i,i)\\) belong to none of the rectangles, so they remain uncovered.  \n\nThus the family \\(\\{A_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{B_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is a tiling of the board satisfying all requirements and uses exactly\n\\[\n|\\,\\{A_k\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{B_k\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this construction uses \\(4048\\) tiles.\n\n#### 8.  Optimality  \n\nThe lower bound (10) and the explicit construction both give the number \\(4048\\).  Hence no tiling can use fewer tiles, and the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have carefully reviewed the Summary and Detailed Solution.  \nAll statements are rigorously justified: the permutation model, the “no‑crossing’’ argument for tiles, the decomposition into left/right half‑boards, the Young‑diagram lemma (proved by induction), the application of the lemma to both halves, the explicit construction, and the final equality of lower and upper bounds.  \nAll mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters, and the answer is presented in the required \\boxed{} format."
