 number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares whose rows are numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and whose columns are numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThe same condition for columns forces \\(\\sigma\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1).\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) belongs to the *left* part of row \\(i\\) (the cells left of the uncovered one).  \nIf a tile \\(T\\) contains \\(L_i\\), then the column interval of \\(T\\) must be a contiguous set of columns that ends at \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\); otherwise the tile would also contain column \\(\\sigma(i)\\), which is the uncovered square of row \\(i\\).  \nHence the **rightmost column** of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).\n\nBecause the numbers \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) are distinct (the \\(\\sigma(i)\\) are a permutation), two different cells \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) have different rightmost columns.  \nConsequently a single tile cannot contain two distinct cells of the form \\(L_i\\).  \n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) (the unique row for which \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) has no left part).  \nThus at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover all cells \\(L_i\\).  \nWe have proved:\n\n\\[\n\\text{(Lemma 1)}\\qquad\\text{Any admissible tiling uses at least }n-1\\text{ tiles to cover the left parts.}\n\\]\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the *right* parts yields a second set of mandatory cells.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nIf a tile contains \\(R_i\\), its column interval must start at \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\); otherwise it would contain the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nHence the **leftmost column** of that tile is \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\).  \nSince the numbers \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) are distinct (again because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation), each tile can contain at most one cell \\(R_i\\).  \nThere are \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\); therefore at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are required.\n\nThus we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\text{(Lemma 2)}\\qquad\\text{Any admissible tiling uses at least }n-1\\text{ tiles to cover the right parts.}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑part cells and the right‑part cells lie in disjoint regions of the board, so the two lower bounds add.  \nCombining Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 gives the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every admissible tiling needs at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nBecause the problem is invariant under a permutation of the rows, we may apply the row permutation \\(\\pi(i)=\\sigma(i)\\).  \nUnder this permutation the uncovered squares become the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nIf we can tile the board minus the diagonal with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, then applying the inverse row permutation yields a tiling for the original placement of uncovered squares with the same number of tiles.\n\nAssume henceforth that the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles (covering the left parts).*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge 2\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) tile.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every cell left of the diagonal.\n\n*Vertical tiles (covering the right parts).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(j\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, disjoint from all \\(H_i\\), and together cover every cell right of the diagonal.\n\nAll \\(2n-2\\) tiles are axis‑aligned, do not overlap, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal ones.  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction shows that the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid, while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column, is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
