"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Because each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n2. **Reduction to the identity permutation.**  \n   By permuting the columns of the whole board according to \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) we obtain an isomorphic board in which the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  Row\u2011 and column\u2011permutations preserve the tiling condition, therefore the minimum number of tiles is the same for any \\(\\sigma\\); we may assume \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).\n\n3. **Classification of admissible rectangles.**  \n   For the identity permutation a rectangle covering rows \\(r_1\\le\\cdots\\le r_2\\) and columns \\(c_1\\le\\cdots\\le c_2\\) can contain no diagonal cell only if either  \n\n   * \\(c_2< r_1\\) (the whole rectangle lies **left** of every row it touches), or  \n   * \\(c_1> r_2\\) (the whole rectangle lies **right** of every row it touches).\n\n   Consequently every tile is either a *left\u2011type* tile (covers only cells left of the diagonal) or a *right\u2011type* tile (covers only cells right of the diagonal).  \n\n4. **A lower bound.**  \n   In row \\(i\\;(i\\ge2)\\) the cell \\((i,i-1)\\) is the rightmost uncovered cell on the left side; any left\u2011type tile that contains it must have its rightmost column equal to \\(i-1\\).  Since the numbers \\(i-1\\) are distinct, a left\u2011type tile can contain at most one such cell.  There are \\(n-1\\) rows with a left\u2011side cell, so at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011type tiles are necessary.  \n\n   An analogous argument applied to the right\u2011side cells \\((i,i+1)\\;(i\\le n-1)\\) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011type tiles are needed.  \n\n   Hence any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Keep the diagonal uncovered.  \n\n   *For each row \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a horizontal \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) tile covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\).*  \n   *For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile covering the cells \\((j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\).*  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles are pairwise disjoint and cover every cell left of the diagonal; the \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles are pairwise disjoint, disjoint from the horizontals, and cover every cell right of the diagonal.  Thus exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles cover all squares except the diagonal ones.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  \n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there is a function  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThe same condition for columns forces \\(\\sigma\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to the identity permutation  \n\nPermute the columns of the whole board by the permutation \\(\\tau=\\sigma^{-1}\\).  \nAfter this column permutation the uncovered squares become  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,\\tau(\\sigma(i)))\\mid i\\}= \\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe main diagonal.  \nA column permutation merely re\u2011orders the columns; it does not affect the possibility of placing axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles, nor does it change the requirement \u201cexactly one uncovered square in each row and column\u201d.  \nTherefore the minimum number of tiles for the original board equals the minimum number for the board with diagonal uncovered squares.  From now on we assume  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Geometry of admissible rectangles  \n\nConsider a rectangle (tile) \\(T\\) covering a set of consecutive rows  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{r_1,r_1+1,\\dots ,r_2\\},\\qquad 1\\le r_1\\le r_2\\le n,\n\\]\n\nand a set of consecutive columns  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{c_1,c_1+1,\\dots ,c_2\\},\\qquad 1\\le c_1\\le c_2\\le n.\n\\]\n\nBecause the diagonal cell \\((k,k)\\) is uncovered for every \\(k\\), \\(T\\) may not contain any pair \\((k,k)\\) with \\(k\\in R\\).  Hence for each \\(k\\in R\\) we must have either  \n\n\\[\nc_2<k\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad c_1>k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf the first inequality holds for some \\(k\\) and the second holds for another \\(k'\\), then \\(c_2<k\\le k' <c_1\\), which is impossible because \\(c_1\\le c_2\\).  Consequently **all** rows in \\(R\\) satisfy the same alternative in (1).  \n\n* If they satisfy \\(c_2<k\\) for every \\(k\\in R\\), then \\(c_2<r_1\\).  The whole rectangle lies strictly to the left of the smallest row it occupies; we call such a tile **left\u2011type**.  \n* If they satisfy \\(c_1>k\\) for every \\(k\\in R\\), then \\(c_1>r_2\\).  The rectangle lies strictly to the right of the largest row it occupies; we call it **right\u2011type**.\n\nThus every admissible tile is either left\u2011type (covers only cells left of the diagonal) or right\u2011type (covers only cells right of the diagonal).  No tile can contain both a left\u2011side cell and a right\u2011side cell.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\ge2\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,i-1)\n\\]\n\nis the rightmost cell of that row that lies left of the diagonal.  Any left\u2011type tile containing \\(L_i\\) must have its rightmost column equal to \\(i-1\\) (otherwise the tile would also contain the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\)).  Since the numbers \\(i-1\\) are distinct for different rows, a single left\u2011type tile can contain **at most one** of the cells \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\).  There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells, so at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011type tiles are required.\n\nAnalogously, for each row \\(i\\le n-1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,i+1)\n\\]\n\nis the leftmost cell of that row that lies right of the diagonal.  Any right\u2011type tile containing \\(R_i\\) must have its leftmost column equal to \\(i+1\\); consequently a right\u2011type tile can contain at most one of the cells \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\).  Hence at least another \\(n-1\\) right\u2011type tiles are needed.\n\nBecause left\u2011type and right\u2011type tiles are disjoint, the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nKeep the diagonal cells uncovered.  \n\n*For each row \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle.  The \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(H_2,\\dots ,H_n\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover **all** cells left of the diagonal.\n\n*For each column \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.  The \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, disjoint from all \\(H_i\\), and together cover **all** cells right of the diagonal.\n\nThus the \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\n\\[\n\\{H_2,\\dots ,H_n\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and no two tiles overlap.  Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality and the answer for \\(n=2025\\)  \n\nInequality (2) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of \u00a75 shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.** The smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
