|S_L|=n-1\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(j<n\\) define the square immediately to the right of the hole:\n\\[\nR_j=(\\sigma^{-1}(j),\\,j+1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nS_R=\\{R_j\\mid j<n\\}.\n\\]\nExactly one column has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\), so \\(|S_R|=n-1\\).\n\nA direct check shows that \\(S_L\\cap S_R=\\varnothing\\); indeed if\n\\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)=(\\sigma^{-1}(j),j+1)\\) then \\(i=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) and\n\\(\\sigma(i)-1=j+1\\), which implies \\(j-1=j+1\\), impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemmas on the content of a tile\n\nA tile \\(T=I\\times J\\) is *admissible* if it contains no uncovered square, i.e.\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in I\\;(\\sigma(i)\\notin J). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f1.**  \nAn admissible tile contains at most one element of \\(S_L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(T\\) contains two distinct squares \\(L_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and\n\\(L_k=(k,\\sigma(k)-1)\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(k)-1\\).  \nSince \\(J\\) is an interval of columns, it contains every integer between\n\\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(k)-1\\), in particular the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).\nBecause \\(i\\in I\\), the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in H\\) lies inside \\(T\\),\ncontradicting (4).  Hence no tile can contain two distinct squares of \\(S_L\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f2.**  \nAn admissible tile contains at most one element of \\(S_R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric to Lemma\u202f1, using rows instead of columns. \u220e\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f3.**  \nAn admissible tile cannot contain simultaneously a square of \\(S_L\\) and a square of \\(S_R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(T\\) contains \\(L_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in S_L\\) and\n\\(R_j=(\\sigma^{-1}(j),j+1)\\in S_R\\).  \nThus \\(i,\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\in I\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)-1,\\;j+1\\in J\\).\n\nIf \\(\\sigma(i)-1<j+1\\), then the interval \\(J\\) also contains the column\n\\(\\sigma(i)\\); consequently the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) lies in \\(T\\),\ncontradicting (4).  \n\nIf \\(\\sigma(i)-1>j+1\\), then \\(J\\) contains the column \\(j\\); the uncovered\nsquare \\((\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)\\) then lies in \\(T\\), again contradicting (4).  \n\nThus the two cases are impossible, and the tile cannot contain both types of\nsquares. \u220e\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Each admissible tile contains at most one element of }S_L\\cup S_R.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nAll squares of \\(S_L\\cup S_R\\) belong to the set of squares that must be tiled,\nbecause they are not uncovered (they are not in \\(H\\)).  \nBy (5) each tile can cover at most one of these squares, therefore at least\n\\(|S_L\\cup S_R|\\) tiles are required.  Using \\(|S_L|=|S_R|=n-1\\) and\n\\(S_L\\cap S_R=\\varnothing\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_L\\cup S_R|=|S_L|+|S_R|=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling needs }\\ge 2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i\\in[n]\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nFor each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe rectangles \\(H_i\\) are pairwise disjoint and cover precisely the squares\nstrictly above the diagonal.  \nThe rectangles \\(V_j\\) are pairwise disjoint and cover precisely the squares\nstrictly below the diagonal.  \nNo rectangle contains a diagonal square, so the diagonal squares remain\nuncovered, satisfying the requirement that each row and each column have\nexactly one uncovered unit square.\n\nThe total number of rectangles is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Minimality\n\nInequality (6) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles,\nwhile construction (9) shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
