olumn \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the intersection of column \\(c\\) with the left region is a single interval of rows (by monotonicity of \\(P\\)). Place a vertical tile covering exactly that interval.  \n   - For each row \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the intersection of row \\(r\\) with the right region is a single interval of columns. Place a horizontal tile covering exactly that interval.\n\n   The tiles are disjoint, avoid all holes, and together cover every square that is not a hole. The construction uses at most \\((n-1)\\) vertical tiles and \\((n-1)\\) horizontal tiles, i.e. exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction achieving \\(2n-2\\) coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the set of unit squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nAll other squares are to be covered by rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines; tiles may not overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\sigma(i)-1), &\\text{if }\\sigma(i)>1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined}, &\\text{if }\\sigma(i)=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nR_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\sigma(i)+1), &\\text{if }\\sigma(i)<n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined}, &\\text{if }\\sigma(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_i\\) is the square immediately to the left of the hole in row \\(i\\) (when it exists), and \\(R_i\\) is the square immediately to the right of the hole (when it exists)