ered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Vertical tiles (covering \\(L\\))\n\nFix a column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nBecause the path is monotone, the set of rows \\(i\\) for which the point \\((c,i)\\) lies left of \\(P\\) is a (possibly empty) **contiguous** interval; denote it by \\(I_c\\).  \nPlace a vertical tile covering exactly the squares \\(\\{(i,c)\\mid i\\in I_c\\}\\).  \nIf \\(I_c=\\varnothing\\) we place no tile in column \\(c\\).  \nColumn \\(n\\) never belongs to \\(L\\), so no vertical tile is needed there.  \nThus we obtain at most \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles, all disjoint and all avoiding holes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Horizontal tiles (covering \\(R\\))\n\nFix a row \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nAgain by monotonicity, the set of columns \\(j\\) for which \\((r,j)\\) lies right of \\(P\\) is a contiguous interval; denote it by \\(J_r\\).  \nPlace a horizontal tile covering exactly the squares \\(\\{(r,j)\\mid j\\in J_r\\}\\).  \nIf \\(J_r=\\varnothing\\) we place no tile in row \\(r\\).  \nRow \\(n\\) never belongs to \\(R\\), so no horizontal tile is needed there.  \nThus we obtain at most \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles, also disjoint and hole‑free.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification\n\n*Every square in \\(L\\) belongs to exactly one vertical tile*: by construction the vertical tile for column \\(c\\) contains precisely the squares of column \\(c\\) that lie left of \\(P\\).  \n\n*Every square in \\(R\\) belongs to exactly one horizontal tile*: analogously, the horizontal tile for row \\(r\\) contains precisely the squares of row \\(r\\) that lie right of \\(P\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint because \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint.  \nConsequently the union of all placed tiles covers **all** squares that are not holes, and no tile contains a hole.\n\nThe total number of tiles is at mo