s allowed (sigma(4)=3, col2 <3, so allowed). Row5 col2 allowed. Row3 col2 allowed. So rectangle rows3-5, cols1-2 covers squares: (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1),(5,2). That's 6 squares. Left region also includes (3,3) (since L3 includes col3), (4,1) already covered, (4,2) already covered, (5,3),(5,4) (since L5 includes cols1-4). Actually L5 includes col1-4, we have covered col1-2 for row5, still need (5,3) and (5,4). Also need (1,1) (L1). So remaining squares after covering rectangle rows3-5, cols1-2: left squares: (1,1), (3,3), (5,3), (5,4). That's 4 squares. Could we cover them with 2 rectangles? Possibly rectangle covering row5 columns3-4 (size 1x2) covers (5,3),(5,4). Then rectangle covering (1,1) and (3,3) cannot be combined because they are not aligned (different rows and columns). Could we combine (1,1) with (3,3) via a rectangle covering rows1-3, columns1-3? That rectangle would include (2,2) which is not a left interval (since sigma(2)=1, left interval empty). But (2,2) is not a left interval; is (2,2) allowed? Row2 sigma=1, left interval is empty, so (2,2) is to the right of sigma(2) (since sigma(2)=1, column2 >1). So (2,2) is in right region, not left. So rectangle covering rows1-3, cols1-3 would include (2,2) which is a right region square, but that's okay if we want to cover right region? No, we cannot cover right region squares with left region rectangles because they would cross the hole (2,1) maybe? Let's check: Row2 hole is at (2,1). The rectangle rows1-3, cols1-3 includes (2,1) which is a hole (uncovered square). So cannot include that. So we cannot combine (1,1) and (3,3) in a rectangle without covering a hole. So we need separate rectangles for (1,1) and (3,3). So total rectangles = 1 (big rectangle) + 1 (row5 col3-4) + 1 (1,1) + 1 (3,3) = 4 rectangles for left region. So left region needs at least 4 rectangles. That's still n-1 = 4. So left region cannot be covered with fewer than n-1 rectangles for this permutation.\n\nThus perhaps left region always requires at least n-1 rectangles. Let's try to prove: For any permutation sigma, the left region (set of squares (i,j) with j < sigma(i)) has a property that each column j contains at least one square that is a \"new\" left interval start? Actually each column j appears as left region for all rows i where sigma(i) > j. Since sigma is a permutation, there is exactly one row i0 where sigma(i0) = j. For rows i < i0, sigma(i) may be greater or less than j. For rows i > i0, sigma(i) may be greater or less. So the left region in column j is a set of rows that are not contiguous in general. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover left region is at least the number of rows i such that sigma(i) = 1? Actually each row i with sigma(i) > 1 must have at least one rectangle covering its leftmost square (i,1). The leftmost column j=1 appears in left region for all rows i where sigma(i) > 1. Since sigma(i) > 1 for all rows except the one with sigma(i)=1. So column 1 left region includes n-1 rows (all except the row with sigma(i)=1). These rows are not necessarily contiguous; they could be separated by the row where sigma(i)=1. So to cover column 1 left region, we need at least 2 rectangles if the row with sigma(i)=1 is not at the top or bottom? Actually if the row with sigma(i)=1 is somewhere in the middle, column 1 left region consists of two blocks: rows above that row and rows below that row. So at least 2 rectangles for column 1. If the row with sigma(i)=1 is at top (row1) or bottom (row n), then column1 left region is contiguous (rows 2..n or rows1..n-1). So can be covered by 1 rectangle. Similarly for column 2, the row with sigma(i)=2 splits column2 left region into two blocks. So each column j may require at least 1 rectangle, possibly more if the row with sigma(i)=j splits the set of rows where sigma(i)>j into two blocks. So the total number of rectangles needed to cover left region is at least the number of columns j for which the set of rows where sigma(i)>j