l lower bound is 2*(n-2) + 2 = 2n-2.\n\nIs that lower bound valid? Let's examine: For any row i, the uncovered square at column sigma(i). The squares left of sigma(i) are L_i, right are R_i. The solution claims that any rectangle cannot contain squares from both L_i and R_i because then it would also contain the uncovered square (by Lemma 1). Indeed, if a rectangle contains a square in L_i (col < sigma(i)) and a square in R_i (col > sigma(i)), then the rectangle's column range must include sigma(i). Since the rectangle also includes row i, it would contain the uncovered square (i, sigma(i)), which is forbidden. So any rectangle can only be wholly in L_i or wholly in R_i. That's correct.\n\nThus each row i with both L_i and R_i nonempty (i.e., sigma(i) not at 1 or n) must have at least two rectangles covering its squares: one covering L_i, one covering R_i. However, could a rectangle covering L_i of row i also cover squares in other rows? Possibly, but that wouldn't reduce the count of rectangles needed for that row. The lower bound counts rectangles per row, but a rectangle could cover L_i for multiple rows simultaneously if they are aligned vertically (i.e., a rectangle spanning multiple rows). But the solution's lower bound argument seems to assume each rectangle can only cover squares from a single row? Actually they argue \"at least two distinct rectangles are needed to cover the squares of that row.\" But could a single rectangle cover L_i for multiple rows? Let's examine: Suppose we have rows i and j with uncovered squares at sigma(i) and sigma(j). Could we have a rectangle that covers L_i and L_j simultaneously? L_i is a set of squares in row i from column 1 to sigma(i)-1. L_j is a set of squares in row j from column 1 to sigma(j)-1. A rectangle covering both would need to include rows i through j (or at least those rows) and columns 1 to max(sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)-1). However, such a rectangle would also include squares at (i, sigma(j)-1) or (j, sigma(i)-1) et