ery square that is not an uncovered square.  \n   - Hence a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists.\n\nSince the lower bound equals the construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares whose rows are indexed by \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and whose columns are indexed by the same set.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain precisely one uncovered unit square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the square \\((i,f(i))\\).  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is the set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,f(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a permutation matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lower bound: at least \\(n\\) tiles are necessary\n\nDefine the binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \na_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1,&\\text{if }(i,j)\\notin U,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&\\text{if }(i,j)\\in U .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a_{ij}=1\\) exactly when the unit square \\((i,j)\\) must be covered.  \nIf we order the columns so that \\(f(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (relabel the columns accordingly), then  \n\n\\[\nA=J-I,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Rectangles and Boolean factorisation\n\nA rectangle (axis‑aligned tile) is determined by a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that no uncovered square lies inside it.  \nFor the identity permutation this condition is simply  \n\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe rectangle covers precisely the entries \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\\).\n\nSuppose we use \\(m\\) rectangles \\((R_k,C_k)\\) (\\(k=1,\\dots ,m\\)).  \nDefine binary matrices  \n\n\\[\nB=(b_{ik})_{n\\times m},\\qquad b_{ik}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1,&i\\in R_k,\\\\\n0,&i\\notin R_k,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nC=(c_{kj})_{m\\times n},\\qquad c_{kj}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1,&j\\in C_k,\\\\\n0,&j\\notin C_k .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe Boolean product \\(B\\circ C\\) has entries  \n\n\\[\n(B\\circ C)_{ij}= \\bigvee_{k=1}^{m}(b_{ik}\\wedge c_{kj}),\n\\]\n\nwhich equals \\(1\\) exactly when some rectangle \\((R_k,C_k)\\) contains the pair \\((i,j)\\).  \nHence the condition that the rectangles cover all squares of \\(A\\) and none of the zeros of \\(A\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\nA = B\\circ C .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the smallest possible number of rectangles equals the Boolean rank of \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Rank argument\n\nInterpret the binary matrices \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) as real matrices (still with entries \\(0\\) or \\(1\\)).  \nThe ordinary matrix product satisfies  \n\n\\[\nBC\\;=\\;\\bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{m}b_{ik}c_{kj}\\bigr)_{ij}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\((B\\circ C)_{ij}=1\\) then there exists a \\(k\\) with \\(b_{ik}=c_{kj}=1\\), whence \\((BC)_{ij}\\ge1\\).  \nIf \\((B\\circ C)_{ij}=0\\) then for every \\(k\\) we have \\(b_{ik}c_{kj}=0\\), so \\((BC)_{ij}=0\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nBC = A .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTaking ranks over the real numbers gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le \\operatorname{rank}(B)\\le m .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe matrix \\(A=J-I\\) is invertible: its eigenvalues are \\(n-1\\) (once) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)), none of which is zero. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4)–(5) we obtain \\(m\\ge n\\).  \nTherefore any family of rectangles that covers all the ones of \\(A\\) (i.e. any tiling satisfying the problem’s requirements) must contain at least \\(n\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound: a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFix the permutation \\(f\\) describing the uncovered squares.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{f(i)\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nGeometrically this is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile that occupies the whole row \\(i\\) except the single uncovered square \\((i,f(i))\\).  \n\nProperties of the construction:\n\n* **Disjointness.** The rectangles lie in distinct rows, so no two of them share a unit square.\n* **Coverage.** Every unit square that is not in \\(U\\) belongs to exactly one rectangle, namely the one corresponding to its row.\n* **Uncovered squares.** By construction, each row \\(i\\) contains precisely the uncovered square \\((i,f(i))\\); consequently each column \\(j\\) also contains exactly one uncovered square (the one in row \\(f^{-1}(j)\\)).  \n\nThus the construction satisfies all the conditions of the problem and uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (Section 2) shows that at least \\(n\\) tiles are necessary, and the explicit tiling (Section 3) shows that \\(n\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
