nother lower bound of \\(n-1\\).  \n   Hence any tiling requires at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2(n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower and upper bounds coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)\\).  \n   For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet the board consist of the unit squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of the squares **left** of the uncovered square in each row, and \\(R\\) consists of the squares **right** of it.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\cup_{i,j} \\text{all squares}\\bigr)\\setminus\\{(i,p(i))\\}=L\\cup R,\n\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nConsequently a tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).  \nWe treat \\(L\\); the same reasoning applies to \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Structure of \\(L\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the number of squares of \\(L\\) in that row equals  \n\n\\[\na_i:=p(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\) is a bijection, the multiset \\(\\{a_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThus the rows of \\(L\\) have *distinct* lengths, ranging from \\(0\\) to \\(n-1\\).\n\nFor a fixed column \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)) the squares of \\(L\\) in column \\(c\\) are precisely those rows \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>c\\).  \nBecause the values \\(p(i)\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), exactly \\(n-c\\) rows satisfy \\(p(i)>c\\).  \nHence column \\(c\\) of \\(L\\) contains exactly \\(n-c\\) squares, forming a vertical segment of height \\(n-c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles\n\n*Tilings of \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) take the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}_c:=\\{(i,c)\\mid p(i)>c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has dimensions \\((n-c)\\times1\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(\\mathcal{C}_1,\\dots ,\\mathcal{C}_{\\,n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(L\\).  \nThus \\(L\\) can be tiled with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Tilings of \\(R\\).*  \nAnalogously, for each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{D}_c:=\\{(i,c)\\mid p(i)<c\\},\n\\]\n\na vertical rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal{D}_2,\\dots ,\\mathcal{D}_n\\) tiles \\(R\\) with \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nConsequently the whole board can be tiled with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2024=4048\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound: at least \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are necessary\n\nWe prove that any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles; the same argument applied to \\(R\\) gives the total lower bound.\n\nConsider a row \\(i\\) with \\(a_i>0\\) (i.e. a non‑empty row of \\(L\\)).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=(i,a_i)\n\\]\n\nbe the **rightmost** square of \\(L\\) in that row.  \nBecause the numbers \\(a_i\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), the columns \\(a_i\\) for the \\(n-1\\) non‑empty rows are all distinct.\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) of a tiling of \\(L\\) contains two distinct rightmost squares \\(r_i\\) and \\(r_{i'}\\) with \\(a_i<a_{i'}\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\) must be axis‑parallel, its right side lies at column \\(\