"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Since each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation  \n   \\[\n   p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad n=2025,\n   \\]  \n   such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n2. **Decomposition into left and right parts.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) let  \n   \\[\n   L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \n   R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Set \\(L=\\bigcup_iL_i\\) and \\(R=\\bigcup_iR_i\\).  \n   Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle that covers a square of \\(L\\) cannot contain a square of \\(R\\) (otherwise it would have to cross the uncovered square of that row). Hence a tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound.**  \n   *Left side.*  \n   For each row with \\(p(i)>1\\) the rightmost square of \\(L_i\\) is \\((i,p(i)-1)\\).  \n   Because the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) run through \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), the columns of these rightmost squares are all distinct.  \n   A rectangle cannot contain two of them: if a rectangle contained \\((i,a)\\) and \\((i',a')\\) with \\(a<a'\\), then its right side would be at column \\(\\ge a'\\) and would also contain \\((i,a')\\), which lies either on the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) (if \\(a'=p(i)\\)) or in the right part \\(R\\) (if \\(a'>p(i)\\)). Both are forbidden.  \n   Hence each rectangle of a tiling of \\(L\\) contains at most one rightmost square, and since there are exactly \\(n-1\\) such squares, any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n   *Right side.*  \n   By symmetry, using the leftmost squares of the non\u2011empty rows of \\(R\\) we obtain the same bound: any tiling of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n   Consequently **any** tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]  \n   rectangles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) do the following:\n\n   * If \\(p(i)>1\\), place a horizontal \\(1\\times(p(i)-1)\\) tile covering the squares \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,p(i)-1)\\).  \n   * If \\(p(i)<n\\), place a horizontal \\(1\\times(n-p(i))\\) tile covering the squares \\((i,p(i)+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\).\n\n   These tiles lie entirely in \\(L\\) or \\(R\\) respectively, are pairwise disjoint (different rows never intersect, and within a row the left and right tiles are separated by the uncovered square), and together they cover every square except the uncovered ones.\n\n   Because exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) (no left tile) and exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\) (no right tile), the total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)\\times2+1+1=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet the board consist of the unit squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nTherefore there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Decomposition into left and right parts  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\text{all squares}\\bigr)\\setminus\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=L\\cup R,\n\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nIf a rectangle contains a square of \\(L\\) and a square of \\(R\\), then in the row of the former square the rectangle would have to cross the column \\(p(i)\\) of the uncovered square, which is impossible because that square must remain uncovered. Hence **every rectangle used in a tiling is wholly contained either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).** Consequently a tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the left part  \n\nConsider a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\).  \nIts rightmost covered square in \\(L\\) is  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{p(i)-1\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus the columns of the squares \\(\\{r_i\\mid p(i)>1\\}\\) are all distinct. There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such squares (the unique row with \\(p(i)=1\\) contributes none).\n\n**Claim.** No axis\u2011parallel rectangle can contain two distinct squares \\(r_i\\) and \\(r_{i'}\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) contains \\(r_i=(i,a)\\) and \\(r_{i'}=(i',a')\\) with \\(a<a'\\).  \nWrite \\(Q=[c_1,c_2]\\times [d_1,d_2]\\) where \\([c_1,c_2]\\) is the interval of rows and \\([d_1,d_2]\\) the interval of columns.  \nSince \\(r_i\\in Q\\) and \\(r_{i'}\\in Q\\), we have \\(c_1\\le i,i'\\le c_2\\) and \\(d_1\\le a<a'\\le d_2\\).  \nHence for the row \\(i\\) the rectangle also contains the square \\((i,a')\\) (because its column interval includes \\(a'\\)).  \n\nNow \\(p(i)=a+1\\). Because \\(a'<a+1\\) would contradict \\(a<a'\\), we have \\(a'\\ge a+1=p(i)\\).  \n\n- If \\(a'=p(i)\\), then \\((i,a')\\) is the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\), which must not be covered \u2013 contradiction.  \n- If \\(a'>p(i)\\), then \\((i,a')\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square, i.e. in the set \\(R\\), which cannot belong to a rectangle that is supposed to lie entirely in \\(L\\) \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus such a rectangle cannot exist. \u220e\n\nSince each rectangle of a tiling of \\(L\\) can contain at most one of the \\(n-1\\) squares \\(\\{r_i\\}\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover \\(L\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the right part  \n\nApply the symmetric argument to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=(i,p(i)+1),\\qquad\\text{for rows with }p(i)<n,\n\\]\n\nthe **leftmost** covered squares of \\(R\\). Their columns are the distinct numbers \\(\\{p(i)+1\\mid p(i)<n\\}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\). By the same reasoning as above, any rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one of these squares, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(R\\).\n\nConsequently **any tiling of the whole board uses at least**\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) proceed as follows.\n\n* If \\(p(i)>1\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(p(i)-1)\\) covering the squares  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,p(i)-1).\n\\]\n\n* If \\(p(i)<n\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(n-p(i))\\) covering the squares  \n\n\\[\n(i,p(i)+1),(i,p(i)+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n\\]\n\nThese tiles lie entirely in \\(L\\) (first case) or in \\(R\\) (second case).  \nTwo different rows never share a tile, so the tiles are pairwise disjoint.  \nWithin a given row the left tile (if present) and the right tile (if present) are separated by the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\); therefore they do not overlap.  \n\nAll squares of the board except the uncovered ones are covered, because each square either lies left of its row\u2019s uncovered square (hence belongs to the left tile of that row) or right of it (hence belongs to the right tile).\n\nNow count the tiles. Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\); that row receives only a right tile. Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\); that row receives only a left tile. Every other row (\\(n-2\\) rows) receives both a left and a right tile. Hence the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)\\times2+1+1=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this equals  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(4048\\) tiles exists, and by the lower\u2011bound argument no tiling can use fewer tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid the minimum number is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
