e a tile covering the whole left segment of that row:\n     \\[\n     T_t=\\{(i_t,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le t-1\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\(1\\times(t-1)\\) rectangle.  \n   - These \\(n-1\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(L\\).  \n   - Similarly, for each \\(t=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let \\(j_t\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(j_t)=t\\) and place the tile\n     \\[\n     S_t=\\{(j_t,j)\\mid t+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\(1\\times(n-t)\\) rectangle.  \n   - The \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(\\{S_t\\}\\) cover all squares of \\(R\\) without overlap.  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   - Both regions can be tiled with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles, and no tiling can use fewer.  \n   - Hence the minimum total number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) there is exactly one uncovered unit square; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.  \nThe set of uncovered squares is therefore  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Two disjoint regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of the squares that lie **to the left** of the uncovered square in their row, and \\(R\\) consists of the squares that lie **to the right** of it.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus U = L\\cup R,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nLet a tile be an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior contains no square of \\(U\\).  \nIf a rectangle contained squares from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\), then for some row \\(i\\) it would contain a column \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) and a column \\(j'>\\sigma(i)\\); consequently it would also contain the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and thus the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), a contradiction.  \nHence every tile is completely contained either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\text{(total number of tiles)}=\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L+\\#\\text{tiles covering }R .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe treat the two regions separately.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for one region  \n\nWe prove that any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \nThe same argument applied to \\(R\\) will give the identical bound.\n\nFor a column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, \\(|S_c|=n-c\\); in other words, column \\(c\\) occurs in exactly \\(n-c\\) rows of \\(L\\).\n\nConsider the rightmost column of \\(L\\), namely column \\(n-1\\).  \nSince \\(|S_{\\,n-1}|=1\\), there is a unique row \\(i_n\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_n)=n\\); the only square of \\(L\\) in column \\(n-1\\) is \\((i_n,n-1)\\).  \nAny rectangle covering this square cannot contain any other row, because no other row possesses a square in column \\(n-1\\).  \nThus a distinct rectangle is forced for row \\(i_n\\).\n\nRemove that rectangle from the board.  \nNow look at column \\(n-2\\).  \nThe set of rows that still contain a square in this column is  \n\n\\[\nS_{\\,n-2}\\setminus\\{i_n\\}=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)=n-1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich again has size one.  \nHence a new rectangle, confined to the row whose uncovered square lies in column \\(n-1\\), is necessary.\n\nProceeding inductively: after having placed rectangles for the rows whose uncovered squares lie in columns \\(t+1,\\dots ,n\\) (with \\(t\\ge 1\\)), the only remaining square of column \\(t\\) lies in the unique row \\(i_{t+1}\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_{t+1})=t+1\\).  \nConsequently a fresh rectangle is required for that row.  \n\nThus for each integer \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) we obtain a distinct rectangle, giving at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles in any tiling of \\(L\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any tiling of }L\\text{ uses }\\ge n-1\\text{ rectangles}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning applied to the right region \\(R\\) (now looking at the leftmost column of \\(R\\), i.e. column \\(2\\), and moving rightwards) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any tiling of }R\\text{ uses }\\ge n-1\\text{ rectangles}.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (3) with (1) we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\text{total number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\; (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nWe now exhibit a tiling that uses exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles for each region.\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(i_t\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i_t)=t\\).  \nPlace the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_t=\\{(i_t,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le t-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(t-1)\\) tile lying entirely in \\(L\\).  \nThe rows \\(i_t\\) are distinct, so the rectangles \\(\\{T_t\\}_{t=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery square of \\(L\\) belongs to exactly one of these rectangles, because a square \\((i,j)\\in L\\) satisfies \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\); letting \\(t=\\sigma(i)\\) we have \\((i,j)\\in T_t\\).  \nHence \\(L\\) is tiled by precisely \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each \\(t\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(j_t\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(j_t)=t\\).  \nPlace the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nS_t=\\{(j_t,j)\\mid t+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times(n-t)\\) tile lying entirely in \\(R\\).  \nAgain the rows \\(j_t\\) are distinct, so the rectangles \\(\\{S_t\\}_{t=1}^{n-1}\\) are disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(R\\).\n\nThus we have constructed a tiling of the whole board (except the uncovered squares) that uses exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (4) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
