 i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of all squares that lie **to the left** of the uncovered square in their row, and \\(R\\) consists of all squares that lie **to the right** of it.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n([n]\\times[n])\\setminus U = L\\cup R,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Any tile lies entirely in one of the two regions\n\nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangular tile (its sides lie on grid lines) that does not contain any square of \\(U\\).  \nSuppose \\(T\\) contains a square \\((i,j)\\in L\\) and also a square \\((i,j')\\) with \\(j'>\\sigma(i)\\).  \nSince the rows of a rectangle are contiguous, the whole interval of columns \\([j,j']\\) in row \\(i\\) would belong to \\(T\\); in particular the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) would be covered, contradicting the fact that \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in U\\).  \nHence a tile cannot contain squares on both sides of the uncovered square of any row, and therefore\n\n\\[\n\\text{each tile is completely contained either in }L\\text{ or in }R.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, if a tiling of the board (except the uncovered squares) uses \\(m\\) tiles, we may write  \n\n\\[\nm = m_L+m_R,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m_L\\) (resp. \\(m_R\\)) is the number of tiles lying in \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left region \\(L\\)\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni_k:=\\text{the unique row with }\\sigma(i_k)=k .\n\\]\n\nThe square \\((i_k,k-1)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) because \\(k-1<\\sigma(i_k)=k\\).  \n\nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\subseteq L\\) that covers \\((i_k,k-1)\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) extended to column \\(k\\) in row \\(i_k\\), it would contain the uncovered square \\((i_k,k)\\), which is impossible.  \nThus the **right\u2011most column** of \\(R\\) is at most \\(k-1\\).  \nSince \\((i_k,k-1)\\in R\\), the right\u2011most column is also at least \\(k-1\\); therefore it is exactly \\(k-1\\).\n\nHence for each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a rectangle whose right\u2011most column equals \\(k-1\\).  \nDifferent values of \\(k\\) give different right\u2011most columns, and a rectangle possesses a single right\u2011most column.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\):\n\n\\[\nm_L\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right region \\(R\\)\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nj_k:=\\text{the unique row with }\\sigma(j_k)=k .\n\\]\n\nThe square \\((j_k,k+1)\\) lies in \\(R\\) because \\(k+1>\\sigma(j_k)=k\\).  \n\nIf a rectangle \\(R'\\subseteq R\\) covers \\((j_k,k+1)\\) and extended to column \\(k\\), it would contain the uncovered square \\((j_k,k)\\), a contradiction.  \nThus the **left\u2011most column** of \\(R'\\) is at least \\(k+1\\); since the square \\((j_k,k+1)\\) belongs to \\(R'\\), the left\u2011most column equals exactly \\(k+1\\).\n\nTherefore for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a rectangle whose left\u2011most column is \\(k+1\\).  \nDistinct \\(k\\) give distinct left\u2011most columns, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\):\n\n\\[\nm_R\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound\n\nCombining (3) and (4) with (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\text{ tiles}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{L}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt is a \\(1\\times(\\sigma(i)-1)\\) tile lying entirely in \\(L\\).  \nRows are distinct, so the tiles \\(\\{T_i^{L}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery square of \\(L\\) belongs to exactly one of them, because a square \\((i,j)\\in L\\) satisfies \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) and therefore lies in \\(T_i^{L}\\).  \nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); consequently the family \\(\\{T_i^{L}\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nSimilarly, for each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) place  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{R}=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times\\bigl(n-\\sigma(i)\\bigr)\\) tile lying entirely in \\(R\\).  \nThese tiles are disjoint, cover all of \\(R\\), and there are \\(n-1\\) of them (the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) contributes none).\n\nIn each row the left tile, the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), and the right tile appear consecutively, so the two families never intersect.  \nThus we obtain a tiling of the whole board (except the uncovered squares) with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality and final answer\n\nThe lower bound (5) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
