. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Lower bound for the left side).\n\nAny partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles of the form (2) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\ge k\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1), \\(|S_k|=n-k\\) and there is a unique index \\(i_k\\) with \\(\\ell_{i_k}=k\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nS_{k+1}=S_k\\setminus\\{i_k\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsider a fixed partition of \\(L\\).  By Lemma 1 each rectangle is \\(I\\times[1,t]\\) for some interval \\(I\\) and width \\(t\\).  \nFor \\(t\\ge1\\) let \\(\\mathcal R_t\\) be the set of rectangles whose width is at least \\(t\\).  \nClearly \\(\\mathcal R_{t+1}\\subseteq\\mathcal R_t\\).\n\nThe cells of column \\(t\\) in \\(L\\) are precisely the pairs \\((i,t)\\) with \\(i\\in S_t\\).  \nSince every such cell must belong to some rectangle, the row set \\(S_t\\) is covered by the row intervals of the rectangles in \\(\\mathcal R_t\\).\n\nNow fix \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  By (4) the row \\(i_t\\) (the unique row with \\(\\ell_{i_t}=t\\)) lies in \\(S_t\\) but not in \\(S_{t+1}\\).  \nIf a rectangle covering \\((i_t,t)\\) belonged to \\(\\mathcal R_{t+1}\\), its width would be at least \\(t+1\\) and therefore its column interval would contain column \\(t+1\\).  Because \\(\\sigma(i_t)=\\ell_{i_t}+1=t+1\\), column \\(t+1\\) is the hole of row \\(i_t\\); such a rectangle would contain a hole, contradicting admissibility.  Hence the rectangle covering \\((i_t,t)\\) lies in \\(\\mathcal R_t\\setminus\\mathcal R_{t+1}\\), which is therefore non‑empty.\n\nThus for each \\(t=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) we have \\(\\mathcal R_t\\setminus\\mathcal R_{t+1}\\neq\\varnothing\\).  Counting these disjoint differences yields  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R_1|\\ge (n-1).\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\mathcal R_1\\) is exactly the set of all rectangles used in the partition of \\(L\\).  Hence any such partition needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Lower bound for the right side).\n\nAny partition of \\(R\\) into rectangles of the form (3) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma 2 to the permutation \\(\\sigma' (i)=n+1-\\sigma(i)\\), which swaps left and right sides.  The right lengths \\(r_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\) become the left lengths for \\(\\sigma'\\), and the same argument gives the bound \\(n-1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Global lower bound).\n\nEvery tiling satisfying the problem’s conditions uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set of all covered cells is the disjoint union \\(L\\cup R\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 any tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\); a tile cannot intersect both sides because its column interval would then contain a hole.  \nConsequently the total number of tiles equals the number of tiles covering \\(L\\) plus the number covering \\(R\\).  \nLemmas 2 and 3 give at least \\(n-1\\) tiles for each side, whence at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles overall. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Construction attaining the bound).\n\nFor the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal) there exists a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWhen \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) we have \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\) and \\(r_i=n-i\\).  \n\n- For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  T_j:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which has height \\(j-1\\) and width 1.  The union of the \\(T_j\\) (for \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\)) covers precisely the set \\(L\\); the rectangles are pairwise disjoint and none contains a diagonal hole.\n\n- For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  S_j:=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  