* of the holes.\nClearly \\(L\\cup R\\) is the set of squares to be tiled; \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left part \\(L\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\na_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le a_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); in particular,\nfor every \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a **unique** row, call it \\(i_k\\), with \\(a_{i_k}=k\\).\n\nConsider the cell \\((i_k,k)\\). It belongs to \\(L\\) and is the **rightmost** cell of row \\(i_k\\) that lies in \\(L\\);\nthe cell \\((i_k,k+1)\\) is the hole \\((i_k,p(i_k))\\).\n\nLet \\(R_k\\) be the rectangle of the tiling that contains \\((i_k,k)\\).\nSince a rectangle is a product of a contiguous set of rows and a contiguous set of columns,\nits column interval is \\([c_k,d_k]\\) with \\(c_k\\le k\\le d_k\\).\nIf \\(d_k\\ge k+1\\), then column \\(k+1\\) would also belong to the rectangle,\nand the square \\((i_k,k+1)\\) (the hole) would be covered – impossible.\nHence \\(d_k=k\\); the rectangle’s **rightmost column is exactly \\(k\\)**.\n\nTwo different values of \\(k\\) cannot belong to the same rectangle, because a rectangle has a single rightmost column.\nThus the rectangles \\(\\{R_k\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) are all distinct.\nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right part \\(R\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nb_i:=n-p(i)\\qquad (0\\le b_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\{b_i\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique row,\ndenoted \\(j_k\\), with \\(b_{j_k}=k\\).\n\nThe right interval of row \\(j_k\\) consists of the columns  \n\n\\[\np(j_k)+1,\\;p(j_k)+2,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nand its **leftmost** cell is \\((j_k,p(j_k)+1)=(j_k,n-k+1)\\).\n\nLet \\(S_