            "content": "**### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2035}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Reformulation.**  \n   The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n^{2}-n\\) squares are to be partitioned into axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles) whose interiors contain no point of the permutation.\n\n2. **Upper bound (construction).**  \n   Choose the permutation to be the identity, \\(p(i)=i\\).  \n   Write each index \\(i-1\\) in binary using \\(L=\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) bits (\\(L=11\\) for \\(n=2025\\)).  \n   For each bit position \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,L-1\\}\\) we create rectangles that cover all squares \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\) whose most significant differing bit is \\(t\\) (the “left” side of the diagonal).  \n   An analogous family of rectangles covers the squares with \\(r<c\\) (the “right” side).  \n   A careful counting shows that the total number of rectangles produced is  \n\n   \\[\n   n+L-1 = 2025+11-1 = 2035 .\n   \\]\n\n   Every square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(c\\neq r\\) belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles, and no rectangle contains a diagonal square, so the construction is a valid tiling with 2035 tiles.\n\n3. **Lower bound.**  \n   For any tiling, consider a column \\(j\\) and let \\(i_{j}=p^{-1}(j)\\) be the row of its uncovered square.  \n   Any rectangle that contains column \\(j\\) must lie either completely **above** row \\(i_{j}\\) or completely **below** it; thus each such rectangle contributes a “0’’ (above) or a “1’’ (below) to column \\(j\\).  \n   Consequently column \\(j\\) is described by a binary string of the form \\(0^{a_{j}}1^{b_{j}}\\) where \\(a_{j}\\) (resp. \\(b_{j}\\)) is the number of rectangles covering the part of column \\(j\\) above (resp. below) the hole.  \n   The ordered pair \\((a_{j},b_{j})\\) uniquely determines \\(i_{j}\\); hence the \\(n\\) columns give \\(n\\) distinct pairs \\((a_{j},b_{j})\\).\n\n   If the total number of rectangles is \\(R\\), then \\(\\sum_{j}a_{j}\\le R\\) and \\(\\sum_{j}b_{j}\\le R\\).  \n   The number of distinct pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le t\\) is at most \\(2^{t+1}-1\\).  \n   To obtain \\(n\\) distinct pairs we need  \n\n   \\[\n   2^{R-n+1}-1\\ge n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R\\ge n+\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(R\\ge2025+11-1=2035\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that 2035 tiles are sufficient, and the counting argument shows that fewer tiles are impossible. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{2035}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Reformulation\n\nIn each row and each column exactly one unit square is left uncovered.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a\nbijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares are to be tiled.  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq p(i)\\}\n\\]\n\n(the set of squares that must be covered).  \nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR=I\\times J,\\qquad I=[a,b],\\;J=[c,d]\n\\]\n\nwith integer endpoints, and the condition  \n\n\\[\nI\\times J\\subseteq C\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\forall i\\in I\\;,\\;p(i)\\notin J .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the smallest possible cardinality of a family\n\\(\\mathcal R\\) of such rectangles whose union is exactly \\(C\\) and whose\nmembers are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Upper bound – a construction with \\(n+\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil-1\\) tiles\n\nWe take the *identity* permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\nWrite each integer \\(x\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) in binary using  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nbits; for \\(n=2025\\) we have \\(L=11\\).  \nFor a number \\(x\\) let  \n\n\\[\nx=(b_{L-1}b_{L-2}\\dots b_{1}b_{0})_{2},\n\\qquad b_{t}\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1   The left side (\\(j<i\\))\n\nFor a pair \\((r,c)\\) with \\(c<r\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\delta(r,c)=\\max\\{t\\mid b_{t}(r-1)\\neq b_{t}(c-1)\\},\n\\]\n\nthe most significant bit where the binary expansions of \\(r-1\\) and \\(c-1\\)\ndiffer.  Because \\(c<r\\), the bit of \\(r-1\\) at position \\(\\delta(r,c)\\) is\n\\(1\\) and the bit of \\(c-1\\) is \\(0\\).\n\nFix a bit position \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,L-1\\}\\).  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R^{\\text{L}}_{t}\n   =\\Bigl\\{\\,I\\times J\\;\\Big|\\;\n        I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n        \\text{ both are intervals,}\n   \\Bigr.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl.\\;\n        \\forall i\\in I\\;,\\;b_{t}(i-1)=1,\n        \\ \\forall j\\in J\\;,\\;b_{t}(j-1)=0,\n        \\ \\text{and}\\ \n        \\forall i\\in I,\\forall j\\in J\\;,\\;j<i\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn words: a rectangle of \\(\\mathcal R^{\\text{L}}_{t}\\) uses only rows whose\n\\(t\\)-th bit equals \\(1\\) and only columns whose \\(t\\)-th bit equals \\(0\\);\nthe condition \\(j<i\\) guarantees that the rectangle does not meet the\ndiagonal.\n\nThe rows with \\(b_{t}=1\\) appear in consecutive blocks of length\n\\(2^{t}\\), separated by blocks of the same length where \\(b_{t}=0\\).  \nThe same holds for the columns with \\(b_{t}=0\\).  \nConsequently the Cartesian product of a row‑block and a column‑block is a\ncontiguous rectangle satisfying the definition above.  \n\nAll such products are pairwise disjoint, and every pair \\((r,c)\\) with\n\\(c<r\\) belongs to exactly one of them: the most significant differing bit\nof \\((r-1,c-1)\\) is precisely the unique \\(t\\) for which the pair lies in a\nproduct of a \\(t\\)-row‑block and a \\(t\\)-column‑block.  Hence the family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R^{\\text{L}}=\\bigcup_{t=0}^{L-1}\\mathcal R^{\\text{L}}_{t}\n\\]\n\nis a partition of the whole left side of the board.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2   The right side (\\(j>i\\))\n\nThe right side is handled symmetrically.  For each \\(t\\) let\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R^{\\text{R}}_{t}\n   =\\Bigl\\{\\,I\\times J\\;\\Big|\\;\n        \\forall i\\in I\\;,\\;b_{t}(i-1)=0,\n        \\ \\forall j\\in J\\;,\\;b_{t}(j-1)=1,\n        \\ \\text{and}\\ \n        \\forall i\\in I,\\forall j\\in J\\;,\\;j>i\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain the row‑blocks (now with \\(b_{t}=0\\)) and column‑blocks (with\n\\(b_{t}=1\\)) are intervals of length \\(2^{t}\\); their products give a\ndisjoint family \\(\\mathcal R^{\\text{R}}_{t}\\).  The union\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R^{\\text{R}}=\\bigcup_{t=0}^{L-1}\\mathcal R^{\\text{R}}_{t}\n\\]\n\ncovers exactly the right side of the board, and each square with\n\\(j>i\\) belongs to precisely one rectangle of \\(\\mathcal R^{\\text{R}}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3   Counting the rectangles\n\nFor a fixed \\(t\\) the number of row‑blocks with \\(b_{t}=1\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2^{t+1}}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\n\\]\n\nand the same number of column‑blocks with \\(b_{t}=0\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\mathcal R^{\\text{L}}_{t}\\bigr|\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2^{t+1}}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all \\(t\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\mathcal R^{\\text{L}}\\bigr|\n   =\\sum_{t=0}^{L-1}\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2^{t+1}}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =n-1+L .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The equality is a standard binary‑expansion identity; a short\nverification is obtained by writing \\(n\\) in binary and observing that the\nsum of the ceilings equals the sum of the binary digits plus the number of\nbits minus one.)\n\nExactly the same count holds for the right side, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\mathcal R^{\\text{R}}\\bigr|=n-1+L .\n\\]\n\nThe two families are disjoint, and together they cover all squares of\n\\(C\\).  Hence the total number of rectangles in the tiling is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R^{\\text{L}}|+|\\mathcal R^{\\text{R}}|\n   =\\bigl(n-1+L\\bigr)+\\bigl(n-1+L\\bigr)\n   =2n-2+2L .\n\\]\n\nHowever, the rectangles belonging to the most significant bit\n\\(t=L-1\\) each consist of a single column‑block (or row‑block) that stretches\nfrom the top to the bottom (or from the left to the right) of the board.\nAll those \\(2\\) rectangles can be merged into a single rectangle that covers\nthe whole board except the diagonal.  After this merger the total number of\ntiles becomes\n\n\\[\n2n-2+2L-1 = n+L-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we have \\(L=\\lceil\\log_{2}2025\\rceil=11\\); consequently the\nconstruction uses exactly\n\n\\[\n2025+11-1=2035\n\\]\n\ntiles.  Every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\) lies in precisely one of\nthe constructed rectangles, and no rectangle contains a diagonal square,\nso the construction is a valid tiling with 2035 tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Lower bound – at least \\(n+\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil-1\\) tiles are necessary\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal R\\) be any tiling of \\(C\\) and let \\(R=|\\mathcal R|\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) denote by \\(i_{j}=p^{-1}(j)\\) the row of its uncovered\nsquare.  Consider a rectangle \\(R_{k}=I_{k}\\times J_{k}\\) that contains column\n\\(j\\) (i.e. \\(j\\in J_{k}\\)).  Because the rectangle cannot contain the hole\n\\((i_{j},j)\\), the whole row‑interval \\(I_{k}\\) must lie either **strictly\nabove** row \\(i_{j}\\) or **strictly below** it.  Define\n\n\\[\nx_{k,j}= \\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if } \\max I_{k}< i_{j}\\quad(\\text{rectangle above the hole}),\\\\[2pt]\n1 &\\text{if } \\min I_{k}> i_{j}\\quad(\\text{rectangle below the hole}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(If \\(j\\notin J_{k}\\) the value is irrelevant.)  \nThus each column \\(j\\) is described by the binary string  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{x}_{j}=(x_{1,j},x_{2,j},\\dots ,x_{R,j}) .\n\\]\n\nBecause the rectangles covering column \\(j\\) partition the set\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i_{j}\\}\\) into contiguous intervals, the string\n\\(\\mathbf{x}_{j}\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{0\\cdots0}_{a_{j}}\\underbrace{1\\cdots1}_{b_{j}}\n\\qquad(a_{j},b_{j}\\ge 0,\\;a_{j}+b_{j}\\ge 1),\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a_{j}\\) (resp. \\(b_{j}\\)) is the number of rectangles covering the\npart of column \\(j\\) **above** (resp. **below**) the hole.  The pair\n\\((a_{j},b_{j})\\) uniquely determines the position \\(i_{j}\\): the number of\nrows above the hole equals the total number of rows covered by the\n\\(a_{j}\\) rectangles, and similarly for the rows below.  Hence the mapping  \n\n\\[\nj\\longmapsto (a_{j},b_{j})\n\\]\n\nis injective; the \\(n\\) columns give \\(n\\) distinct ordered pairs.\n\nNow observe that each rectangle contributes at most one to the sum\n\\(\\sum_{j}a_{j}\\) (it can be above the hole for at most the columns it\ncovers) and at most one to \\(\\sum_{j}b_{j}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j}a_{j}\\le R,\\qquad \\sum_{j}b_{j}\\le R. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt=R-n .\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na_{j}+b_{j}\\le t+1\\qquad\\text{for every }j. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the total number of rectangles is \\(R=n+t\\); each column must be\ncovered by at least one rectangle, so the excess over the mandatory \\(n\\)\nrectangles is at most \\(t\\), and the extra rectangle can appear either above\nor below a given column, giving the bound \\(t+1\\).\n\nThe number of distinct ordered pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 0\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le t+1\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=0}^