og2 n) - 1, which for n=2025 gives 2035.\n\nWe need to check if that is indeed the minimum. This is reminiscent of known results about covering the complement of a permutation matrix with rectangles: The minimum number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to cover all cells except a permutation matrix is n + ceil(log2 n) - 1. This is known as the \"grid covering problem\" or \"covering the complement of a permutation matrix with rectangles\". There is known result: The minimum number of rectangles needed to cover an n x n board with a permutation of holes is n + floor(log2 n). Actually there is known result: The minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all cells except a rook placement (one per row and column) is n + ceil(log2 n) - 1. I recall a known problem: \"Given an n x n board with one square removed from each row and column (i.e., a permutation of missing squares), what is the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover the remaining squares?\" The answer is n + ceil(log2 n) - 1. This is known as the \"rook problem\" or \"covering with rectangles\". So the solution's answer is plausible.\n\nNow we need to check if the solution's reasoning is valid. The solution uses a construction with n-1 horizontal rectangles covering squares to the right of each hole, plus L rectangles covering the left side using a recursive construction based on powers of two. Then they also provide a lower bound using a set S of size n-1+L such that any rectangle covering two of those squares must also contain a diagonal square, which is forbidden. So each tile can contain at most one of those squares, so at least |S| tiles needed.\n\nWe need to verify that the lower bound argument is valid: They define S1 = {(i,i+1) for i=1,...,n-1} and S2 = {(2^k+1, 2^k) for 0 <= k <= L-1}. Then they claim any axis-parallel rectangle containing two distinct squares from S must also contain a diagonal square (t,t). Let's check each case:\n\n- Both in S1: squares (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i<j. The minimal rectangle covering them is rows i..j+1, columns i+1..j+1. Indeed that rectangle includes (i+1,i+1) which is diagonal. So correct.\n\n- Both in S2: squares (2^k+1,2^k) and (2^ℓ+1,2^ℓ) with k<ℓ. Minimal rectangle rows 2^k+1..2^ℓ+1, columns 2^k..2^ℓ. This includes (2^ℓ,2^ℓ) which is diagonal. Yes.\n\n- One from S1 and one from S2: Let's examine. Suppose (i,i+1) and (2^k+1,2^k). Two cases: i+1 <= 2^k, then rectangle rows i..2^k+1, columns i+1..2^k includes (i+1,i+1) diagonal. If i+1 > 2^k, then i >= 2^k, rectangle rows 2^k+1..i+1, columns 2^k..i+1 includes (2^k+1,2^k+1) diagonal. So correct.\n\nThus any rectangle covering two distinct S elements must contain a diagonal square. Since diagonal squares are uncovered (holes), any tile cannot contain a diagonal square (since tiles cannot cover uncovered squares). So each tile can contain at most one element of S. Since we have |S| = (n-1)+L = n+ceil(log2 n)-1 elements, we need at least that many tiles. This is a valid lower bound.\n\nNow the construction: They define permutation pi as a cyclic shift of length m, where m = 2^{L-1} is the largest power of two not exceeding n. For n=2025, L=11 => 2^{10}=1024, m=1024. So pi(i) = i+m for i <= n-m = 2025-1024 = 1001; for i > n-m (i from 1002 to 2025), pi(i) = i-(n-m) = i-1001. So pi is a cyclic shift by 1024 positions. So each row i has a hole at column pi(i). Then they define H_i = { (i,j) | pi(i) < j <= n }, i.e., squares to the right of the hole in row i. They claim these H_i for i=1..n-1 are pairwise disjoint and cover all squares with j > pi(i). Let's check: For each row i, H_i is a horizontal rectangle covering columns pi(i)+1 through n. Since each row is distinct, the rectangles are disjoint across rows (they occupy distinct rows). So they are disjoint. They cover all squares to the right of each hole. Since each row has exactly one hole, the squares to the right of the hole are covered. So they cover all squares with j > pi(i). Good.\n\nNow the left side squares are those with j <= pi(i). They cover those using L rectangles via recursion. They define R0 = { (i,j) | m+1 <= i <= n, 1 <= j <= m }. So rows from m+1 to n (i.e., rows 1025..2025) and columns 1..m (1..1024). This rectangle is product of rows [m+1,n] and columns [1,m]. Since pi(i) = i+m for i <= n-m (i <= 1001) and pi(i) = i-1001 for i > 1001. For rows i > 1001 (i.e., i >= 1002), pi(i) = i-1001. For i >= 1002, pi(i) = i-1001. Since i >= 1002, pi(i) >= 1. For i up to n, pi(i) <= m? Let's compute: For i = n = 2025, pi(2025) = 2025-1001 = 1024 = m. So for rows i = 1002..2025, pi(i) ranges from 1..1024. So for those rows, j <= pi(i) means j <= some number <= m. So the rectangle R0 includes all squares in rows i >= m+1 (i.e., i >= 1025) and columns j <= m. But note that rows i from 1002..1024 are not included in R0 (since R0 rows start at m+1 = 1025). So squares in rows 1002..1024 and columns <= pi(i) are not covered by R0. Those are left to be covered by recursion on the sub-board of size m x m (first m rows and first m columns). The recursion proceeds similarly: inside the sub-board formed by rows 1..m and columns 1..m, the uncovered squares are again of the same type (a cyclic shift of length m/2? Actually the same permutation restricted to first m rows). Since m is a power of two, we can apply the same construction recursively. The recursion stops after L-1 further steps, producing rectangles R1,...,R_{L-1}. So total L rectangles covering left side.\n\nThus total tiles = (n-1) + L = n + ceil(log2 n) - 1. So the construction yields that many tiles.\n\nThus the solution's reasoning appears valid. The lower bound uses a specific set S of size n+L-1 and shows each tile can contain at most one element of S, thus at least that many tiles needed. This is a standard argument.\n\nThus answer 2035 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check for any presentation issues: The solution is fairly detailed. It provides explicit construction details: defines permutation, defines H_i rectangles, defines R0 rectangle, mentions recursion, but does not explicitly give formulas for R1,...,R_{L-1}. However, it says \"the recursion stops after L-1 further steps, producing rectangles R1,R2,...,R_{L-1}\". It does not give explicit definitions for those rectangles, but it indicates they are constructed similarly. This is a justification gap: the construction for the left side is described recursively but not fully spelled out. However, the solution does provide enough detail to understand the approach: the recursion on a power-of-two sized sub-board. According to the guidelines, this is a Justification Gap (acceptable). The lower bound argument is explicit and seems correct.\n\nNow we need to check for any critical errors in logic: The solution's claim that the rectangles H_i for i=1..n-1 are pairwise disjoint and cover all squares with j > pi(i) is correct. However, note that H_n is not defined; they only define H_i for i=1..n-1. For row n, the hole is at pi(n) = ... Actually pi(n) = pi(2025) = 2025-1001 = 1024. So row n has a hole at column 1024. The squares to the right of that hole are columns 1025..2025. But H_n is not defined. However, they claim that the rectangles H_1,...,H_{n-1} already cover all squares with j > pi(i). Does row n's squares to the right of its hole get covered? Let's check: For row n, pi(n) = 1024. The squares to the right are columns 1025..2025. Are those covered by any H_i? H_i for i=1..n-1 cover squares in rows 1..n-1 only. So row n's right side squares are not covered by any H_i. But perhaps they are covered by the left side rectangles? Actually left side rectangles cover squares with j <= pi(i). For row n, pi(n) = 1024, so squares with j <= 1024 are covered by left side rectangles (R0 includes rows m+1..n and columns 1..m, which includes row n and columns 1..1024). So left side covers columns 1..1024 for rows 1025..2025. Row n is 2025, which is > m+1, so row n's squares with j <= 1024 are covered by R0. But row n's squares with j > 1024 (i.e., columns 1025..2025) are not covered by R0. Are they covered by any other rectangle? The recursion on the sub-board of size m x m only covers rows 1..m and columns 1..m. So row n's right side squares are not covered. Wait, maybe the H_i includes i = n as well? The solution says \"The rectangles H_1,...,H_{n-1} are pairwise disjoint, none of them contains a hole, and together they cover all squares (i,j) with j > pi(i). Hence we already use n-1 tiles.\" Actually they claim that the H_i for i=1..n-1 cover all squares with j > pi(i). But for i=n, j > pi(n) squares are not covered. However, maybe they consider that for i=n, pi(n) = something such that there are no squares with j > pi(n) because pi(n) = n? Let's compute: pi(i) = i+m for i <= n-m, else pi(i) = i-(n-m). For i = n = 2025 > n-m = 1001, so pi(n) = n-(n-m) = n - 1001 = 2025 - 1001 = 1024. So pi(n) = 1024, which is less than n. So there are squares with j > 1024 in row n. So those squares are not covered by H_i for i=1..n-1. So there is a missing tile for row n's right side.\n\nBut perhaps they consider H_n as well but they said H_1,...,H_{n-1} are pairwise disjoint, but maybe they omitted H_n because it's not needed? Let's analyze: The rectangle H_i is defined as { (i,j) | pi(i) < j <= n }. For i = n, pi(n) = 1024, so H_n = { (n,j) | 1024 < j <= n } = { (2025, j) for j = 1025..2025 }. That's a horizontal rectangle covering the right side of row n. It is a valid rectangle. Why would they exclude it? Possibly because they want to avoid counting n rectangles; they want n-1 horizontal rectangles and L left side rectangles, total n-1+L = n+L-1. But if they also need H_n, they'd have n+L rectangles, which would be one more than claimed. However, maybe H_n is covered by some left side rectangle? Let's see: The left side rectangles cover squares with j <= pi(i). For row n, pi(n) = 1024, so left side rectangles cover columns 1..1024. So they don't cover columns 1025..2025. So row n's right side squares are uncovered. So the construction as described seems to miss covering those squares. However, maybe the recursion includes a rectangle that covers those squares? Let's examine the recursion: They define R0 = { (i,j) | m+1 <= i <= n, 1 <= j <= m }. That's rows 1025..2025 and columns 1..1024. So R0 covers left side of rows 1025..2025. Then they consider the sub-board formed by the first m rows and first m columns (rows 1..1024, columns 1..1024). They apply the same construction recursively to that sub-board. That recursion will produce horizontal rectangles for rows 1..(m-1) (i.e., rows 1..1023) covering squares to the right of the holes in those rows within the sub-board (i.e., columns > pi(i) but within the sub-board). However, note that the holes in rows 1..1024 are at columns pi(i) = i+m (since i <= n-m = 1001, but for i up to 1024, some i > n-m). Let's compute pi(i) for i=1..1024: For i <= 1001, pi(i) = i+1024. For i > 1001 (i = 1002..1024), pi(i) = i-1001. So for i in 1002..1024, pi(i) is between 1 and 23. So the holes for rows 1002..1024 are in the left side of the sub-board (columns 1..23). So the recursion will treat the sub-board of size 1024x1024 with holes at positions (i, pi(i)). The recursion will again define horizontal rectangles for rows 1..(m-1) within that sub-board, covering squares to the right of holes within the sub-board. However, the recursion's horizontal rectangles will be limited to columns <= m (since the sub-board is only columns 1..m). So for rows i where pi(i) > m (i.e., i <= 1001), the holes are at columns > m (i+1024 > 1024). Actually for i <= 1001, pi(i) = i+1024 > 1024, so those holes are outside the sub-board's columns (since sub-board only includes columns 1..1024). So for those rows, the sub-board does not contain the hole; the hole is outside the sub-board. So the recursion's horizontal rectangles for those rows will cover all squares in that row within the sub-board (since there is no hole inside the sub-board). But those squares are columns 1..1024. However, those squares are already covered by R0 for rows 1025..2025, but not for rows 1..1001. Wait, we need to examine the entire covering scheme.\n\nLet's step back: The construction aims to cover all squares except the holes. They propose to cover squares to the right of each hole using horizontal rectangles H_i for i=1..n-1. Then they cover the left side squares using L rectangles (including R0 and recursion). However, they omitted H_n. Perhaps they intend that H_n is not needed because the left side rectangles cover the right side squares of row n? That seems unlikely. Let's examine the left side rectangles: R0 covers rows m+1..n (1025..2025) and columns 1..m (1..1024). So for row n (2025), R0 covers columns 1..1024. The right side of row n (columns 1025..2025) is not covered. The recursion on the sub-board (rows 1..1024, columns 1..1024) does not involve row n. So row n's right side squares remain uncovered. So the construction as described is incomplete.\n\nBut perhaps the horizontal rectangles H_i are defined for i=1..n, not n-1, but they wrote n-1 incorrectly. Let's examine the phrase: \"The rectangles H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1} are pairwise disjoint, none of them contains a hole, and together they cover **all** squares (i,j) with j>\\pi(i). Hence we already use n-1 tiles.\" This suggests they think that the squares with j > pi(i) are covered by H_i for i=1..n-1, and perhaps there are no squares with j > pi(n) because pi(n) = n? But pi(n) is not n. So maybe they made a mistake in the definition of pi: perhaps they intended a cyclic shift of length m where m = 2^{L-1} = 1024, but they might have defined pi(i) = i+m mod n (i.e., pi(i) = i+m if i+m <= n, else i+m-n). That would be a cyclic shift. Indeed, they defined pi(i) = i+m for 1 <= i <= n-m, and pi(i) = i-(n-m) for n-m+1 <= i <= n. That is exactly a cyclic shift by m (i.e., pi(i) = i+m mod n). For i = n-m+1 = 1002, pi(1002) = 1002-(n-m) = 1002-1001 = 1. So pi(1002) = 1. For i = n = 2025, pi(2025) = 2025-1001 = 1024. So pi(n) = 1024, not n. So there are squares with j > pi(n). So H_n would be needed.\n\nThus the solution's claim that H_1,...,H_{n-1} cover all squares with j > pi(i) is false: they miss the row n's right side. However, perhaps they intend that H_n is not needed because the rectangle covering the left side includes row n's right side as part of a larger rectangle? Let's examine R0: rows 1025..2025, columns 1..1024. That does not include row n's right side. The recursion on sub-board of size 1024x1024 does not include row n. So row n's right side squares are uncovered.\n\nThus there appears to be a flaw in the construction: they omitted one horizontal rectangle for the last row. However, maybe they intend that the recursion includes a rectangle that covers the right side of row n as part of a larger rectangle that includes rows beyond m? Let's examine the recursion more carefully: They say: \"Inside the sub‑board formed by the first $m$ rows and the first $m$ columns the uncovered squares are again of the same type, with the permutation being the restriction of $\\pi$ to $\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}$. Since $m$ is a power of two, we may apply the same construction to this $m\\times m$ board. The recursion stops after $L-1$ further steps, producing rectangles $R_1,R_2,\\dots ,R_{L-1}$.\"\n\nThus the recursion only deals with rows 1..m and columns 1..m. It does not involve rows > m. So the only rectangles covering rows > m are R0. So the right side of row n is uncovered.\n\nThus the construction as described is incomplete and fails to cover all squares except the holes. However, perhaps the missing rectangle H_n is considered part of the \"n-1 horizontal rectangles\" because they count H_n as part of the left side rectangles? But left side rectangles are vertical rectangles covering left side of rows > m. So no.\n\nThus there is a critical error in the construction: they omitted a rectangle covering the right side of the last row. However, they might have intended to include H_n as part of the horizontal rectangles, but mistakenly wrote n-1 instead of n. If we include H_n, the total number of tiles would be n + L = 2025 + 11 = 2036, not 2035. But perhaps they can reduce one rectangle elsewhere, e.g., combine H_n with some other rectangle? Let's see if we can combine H_n with some other rectangle to keep total count at n+L-1. Could we combine H_n with some left side rectangle? Possibly if we extend R0 to include columns up to n, but that would include holes. So not possible. Could we combine H_n with some other horizontal rectangle? Horizontal rectangles are in distinct rows, cannot combine. So we need an extra tile for row n's right side. So the construction as described yields at least n + L tiles, not n+L-1. But maybe there is a way to avoid using H_n: maybe the hole in row n is at column n (i.e., pi(n) = n) if we choose a different permutation. But they defined pi as a cyclic shift of length m, which does not place a hole at column n for any row. However, we could choose a permutation where the hole in row n is at column n, then H_n would be empty (no squares to the right). But then the left side would need to cover squares to the left of that hole (i.e., all columns except column n). That might be covered by the left side rectangles. But does their construction allow that? They defined pi as a cyclic shift of length m, which is a specific permutation. Could we choose a different permutation that yields the same total number of tiles but avoids needing H_n? Possibly. But they claim the construction works for any n, using this specific pi. The claim that they need n-1 horizontal rectangles seems to assume that the last row's hole is at the rightmost column, so H_n is empty. But with the cyclic shift, the hole is not at the rightmost column. So there is a mismatch.\n\nLet's examine the possibility that they intended to define H_i for i=1..n, but they wrote n-1 inadvertently. If we include H_n, the total number of tiles would be n + L, which would be 2036. However, they claim the minimum is 2035, and the lower bound they prove is n+L-1. If the construction actually uses n+L tiles, then the lower bound would be less than the construction, but the lower bound is n+L-1, which would be less than the construction, so the construction would not be optimal. However, the lower bound is proven to be n+L-1, which is less than the construction's count if the construction uses n+L. That would not prove optimality. But maybe the construction can be tweaked to use n+L-1 by adjusting the permutation such that one of the horizontal rectangles can be merged with a left side rectangle, reducing total count by 1. The solution does not show that.\n\nThus there is a potential flaw in the construction's completeness. However, we need to decide if this flaw is a critical error in reasoning (Level 2) or just a justification gap (Level 3). The construction claims to cover all squares with j > pi(i) using H_i for i=1..n-1. If that claim is false, then the construction is incomplete, i.e., the reasoning that the construction uses exactly n-1 horizontal rectangles is invalid. This is not just a missing detail; it's a false claim about coverage. That could be considered a critical error in reasoning, because the construction does not actually tile the board as claimed. However, we need to see if the claim \"The rectangles H_1,...,H_{n-1} ... cover all squares (i,j) with j > pi(i)\" is false. Let's verify: For i=n, there are squares with j > pi(n). Are those squares covered by any H_i? No, because H_i only cover rows i, not row n. So those squares are not covered. So the claim is false. Therefore the construction is incomplete.\n\nBut maybe the recursion also covers those squares: The recursion on the sub-board of size m x m might produce rectangles that extend beyond column m, covering squares in rows > m? No, recursion is only on rows 1..m. So not.\n\nThus the construction fails to cover squares to the right of the hole in the last row. This is a critical error in the construction. However, the lower bound argument is still valid. So the answer 2035 might still be correct, but the solution's construction is flawed. The lower bound shows that at least 2035 tiles are needed. The solution's construction claims to achieve 2035 but fails to cover all squares, so it does not achieve the bound. Therefore the solution does not actually prove that 2035 is achievable. The answer might still be correct, but the solution does not correctly demonstrate it. According to the hierarchical decision tree, we need to evaluate reasoning validity. The reasoning includes a construction that is claimed to achieve the bound, but the construction is flawed. This is a reasoning error. Does that constitute an invalid method? The method is a constructive method, but the construction is incomplete. The claim that the construction uses n-1 horizontal rectangles is false. This is a mistake in the reasoning. According to Level 2, we need to check if the solution uses valid mathematical principles. The principle used (construction) is valid, but the execution is flawed. Is that considered an invalid method or a critical error in the reasoning? The guidelines say Level 2 is about checking whether the solution uses valid mathematical principles, not about correctness of each claim. However, if the method is applied incorrectly, leading to a false claim, does that constitute an invalid method? The guidelines say: \"If reasoning uses invalid principles (trial-and-error, nonsense, circular reasoning) → FAIL Level 2.\" But here the principle is valid (construction). The error is in the execution: a missing rectangle. This is a mistake in the logical chain, not an invalid principle. According to the hierarchical decision tree, Level 2 checks the validity of the methods used, not the correctness of each step. However, the method is \"construction with recursion\" which is a valid method. So Level 2 might be considered PASS. Then Level 3 would evaluate presentation issues, including missing details. The missing rectangle is a gap in the construction. This could be considered a Justification Gap (Level 3). However, the missing rectangle leads to the construction not covering the board fully, which is a more serious error than just a missing detail. The guidelines for Level 3 say: \"Critical Error: demonstrably wrong intermediate calculations that invalidate the logic chain.\" The missing rectangle invalidates the logic chain because the construction fails to tile the board. That is a critical error, not just a justification gap. So we need to decide if the missing rectangle is a \"demonstrably wrong intermediate step\" that invalidates the logic chain.\n\nThe solution claims that the construction covers all squares. This claim is false. So the intermediate step \"The rectangles H_1,...,H_{n-1} ... cover all squares with j > pi(i)\" is demonstrably wrong. That is a critical error at Level 3. However, we need to see if the missing rectangle is a \"critical error\" or just a \"justification gap\". The guidelines define \"Justification Gap\" as missing details, imprecise wording, incomplete verification, but logic is sound. Here the logic is unsound because the construction does not cover all squares. So it's a critical error.\n\nThus the solution fails at Level 3 due to a critical error. However, we need to see if the solution's lower bound argument is correct and if the answer is indeed 2035. The lower bound is correct, but the construction is flawed. The answer might still be correct, but the solution does not provide a correct construction to achieve it. The problem asks to determine the minimum number of tiles. To prove that the minimum is 2035, we need both a lower bound and a construction achieving that bound. The solution fails to provide a valid construction, so the answer is not proven. However, the answer could still be correct, but the solution does not demonstrate it. According to the hierarchical decision tree, we need to check if the solution's reasoning is valid. Since the construction is flawed, the reasoning is not valid. The lower bound is valid, but the upper bound is not correctly established. So the solution does not correctly prove the answer. This is a reasoning error, but does it count as invalid method? The method is valid, but the execution is wrong. According to guidelines, Level 2 is about method validity, not correctness of each claim. However, if the method is applied incorrectly, the reasoning is invalid. The guidelines do not explicitly state that a flawed execution of a valid method leads to Level 2 failure. They say Level 2 checks if the solution uses valid mathematical principles. The solution uses a constructive method, which is a valid principle. So Level 2 would be PASS. Then Level 3 checks presentation issues. The missing rectangle is a critical error because it invalidates the claim that the construction uses n-1 horizontal rectangles and covers all squares. This is a demonstrably wrong intermediate step. So Level 3 would be FAIL due to a critical error.\n\nThus final verdict: FAIL.\n\nBut we need to be careful: The solution's lower bound and upper bound are both given. The lower bound is correct. The upper bound is claimed but flawed. The answer is correct if there exists some other construction achieving 2035, but the solution does not provide one. The solution's claim of achieving 2035 is not substantiated. According to the hierarchical decision tree, we evaluate the solution's reasoning, not the truth of the answer. Since the solution's reasoning is flawed (construction incomplete), we must mark it as FAIL.\n\nThus final verdict: FAIL.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON with the required fields: Level 1 Result, Level 2 Result, Level 3 Result (list issues), Final Verdict, Reasoning.\n\nWe need to include the final answer from the solution: 2035. Level 1: Is answer correct? The answer is correct (the minimum number is indeed 2035). However, we need to verify if the answer is indeed correct. We have not proven it, but we can accept that the answer is correct based on known results. The solution's answer is correct. So Level 1: CORRECT.\n\nLevel 2: Reasoning uses valid mathematical principles? The solution uses constructive method and lower bound via combinatorial argument. These are valid principles. So Level 2: VALID.\n\nLevel 3: Presentation issues. Identify critical errors: The construction fails to cover the right side squares of the last row, making the claim that the construction uses n-1 horizontal rectangles covering all squares with j > pi(i) false. This is a demonstrably wrong intermediate step that invalidates the logic chain. So it's a Critical Error.\n\nThus Level 3: Critical Error found.\n\nThus final verdict: FAIL.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON with fields:\n\n{\n  \"Level 1 Result\": \"CORRECT (answer 2035)\",\n  \"Level 2 Result\": \"VALID (uses constructive method and combinatorial lower bound)\",\n  \"Level 3 Result\": \"Critical Error: construction incomplete; missing coverage of squares to the right of the hole in the last row, claim that H_1,...,H_{n-1} cover all squares with j > pi(i) is false.\",\n  \"Final Verdict\": \"FAIL\",\n  \"Reasoning\": \"Answer is correct, methods are valid, but the construction is flawed, leading to a critical error in the proof.\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure we follow the output format exactly as required. The instruction says \"Return your verdict as JSON matching the provided schema.\" The schema is not explicitly given, but we can infer from the \"Output Format\" part: Provide a concise summary:\n\n- Level 1 Result: state whether final answer is CORRECT or WRONG (quote the answer)\n- Level 2 Result: state whether reasoning uses VALID or INVALID mathematical principles\n- Level 3 Result: list any presentation issues found (Justification Gaps or Critical Errors in logic chain)\n- Final Verdict: \"PASS\"