& j=\\pi(i),\\\\[2pt]\n1,& j\\neq\\pi(i).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) records exactly the squares that must be covered.  \nWrite \\(J\\) for the all‑ones matrix and \\(P\\) for the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\); then  \n\n\\[\nA = J-P. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a linear relation \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i\\,\\text{row}_i(A)=0\\).  \nFor a fixed column \\(j\\) the entry in that column of the left‑hand side equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i a_{ij}\n= \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i\\Bigr)-\\alpha_{\\pi^{-1}(j)},\n\\]\n\nbecause all rows contribute \\(1\\) except row \\(\\pi^{-1}(j)\\), whose entry is \\(0\\).  \nSince the whole vector is zero, the above expression must be \\(0\\) for every \\(j\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{\\pi^{-1}(j)}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i\\qquad\\text{for all }j.\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\alpha_i\\) are equal to the same constant \\(c:=\\sum_{i}\\alpha_i\\).  \nBut then \\(c=n c\\), so \\((n-1)c=0\\) and consequently \\(c=0\\).  \nHence all \\(\\alpha_i=0\\), proving that the rows are linearly independent and \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** If the board is tiled by \\(r\\) rectangles, then \\(r\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach rectangle \\(I_k\\times J_k\\) corresponds to the rank‑1 matrix  \n\n\\[\nM_k = \\mathbf{1}_{I_k}\\,\\mathbf{1}_{J_k}^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{1}_{I_k}\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_{J_k}\\)) is the characteristic column vector of \\(I_k\\) (resp. \\(J_k\\)).  \nBecause the rectangles are disjoint and together cover exactly the \\(1\\)-entries of \\(A\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nA = \\sum_{k=1}^{r} M_k .\n\\]\n\nThe rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks, and each \\(M_k\\) has rank \\(1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\le \\sum_{k=1}^{r}\\operatorname{rank}(M_k)=r .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(r\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma 1, \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\); Lemma 2 therefore yields  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge n=2025. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Conclusion  \n\nInequalities (2) and (4) together give  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2025.\n\\]\n\nHence Matilda can achieve the required covering with exactly \\(2025\\) rectangular tiles, and it is impossible to do it with fewer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]"
