 the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound – explicit tiling with \\(2025\\) tiles.**  \n   For each column \\(j\\) place a vertical rectangle covering all squares of that column except the uncovered one \\((\\pi^{-1}(j),j)\\).  \n   The \\(2025\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct columns) and together cover every square that is not uncovered. Hence \\(2025\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n3. **Lower bound – rank argument.**  \n   *Define the covering matrix* \\(A\\) by  \n   \\[\n   a_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n   0,& j=\\pi(i),\\\\[2pt]\n   1,& j\\neq\\pi(i).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Then \\(A = J-P\\), where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(P\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\).  \n\n   **Lemma 1.** \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=2025\\).  \n   *Proof.* Suppose \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i\\,\\text{row}_i(A)=0\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \n   For a fixed column \\(j\\) the \\(j\\)-th entry of this linear combination equals  \n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i a_{ij}= \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i\\Bigr)-\\alpha_{\\pi^{-1}(j)},\n   \\]\n   because all rows contribute a \\(1\\) in column \\(j\\) except row \\(\\pi^{-1}(j)\\), whose entry is \\(0\\).  \n   Since the whole vector is zero, the above expression must be \\(0\\) for every \\(j\\); thus  \n   \\[\n   \\alpha_{\\pi^{-1}(j)}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_i\\qquad\\text{for all }j.\n   \\]\n   Hence every \\(\\alpha_i\\) equals the same constant \\(c:=\\sum_i\\alpha_i\\).  \n   But then \\(c=n c\\), so \\((n-1)c=0\\) and consequently \\(c=0\\). Therefore all \\(\\alpha_i=0\\), proving that the rows are linearly independent and \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\). ∎\n\n   **Lemma 2.** If the board is tiled by \\(r\\) rectangles, then \\(r\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).  \n   *Proof.* A rectangle covering the set of rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is represented by the binary matrix \\(M= \\mathbf{1}_I\\mathbf{1}_J^{\\!\\top}\\), the outer product of the characteristic vectors of \\(I\\) and \\(J\\).  Such a matrix has rank \\(1\\).  \n   Because the rectangles are disjoint and together cover exactly the \\(1\\)-entries of \\(A\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   A=\\sum_{k=1}^{r}M_k,\n   \\]\n   where each \\(M_k\\) is rank‑\\(1\\).  Using \\(\\operatorname{rank}(X+Y)\\le\\operatorname{rank}(X)+\\operatorname{rank}(Y)\\) repeatedly,  \n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{r}\\operatorname{rank}(M_k)=r.\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(r\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\). ∎\n\n   Applying Lemma 1 gives \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=2025\\); Lemma 2 therefore yields the lower bound  \n   \\[\n   \\text{number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2025.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction of Step 2 shows that \\(2025\\) tiles are achievable, while Step 3 proves that fewer than \\(2025\\) tiles are impossible.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2025\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The soluti