nstruction with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.**  \n   After the column relabelling we may assume the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\n   \\]  \n   a horizontal rectangle covering the part of row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal, and  \n\n   \\[\n   C_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-1),\n   \\]  \n   a vertical rectangle covering the part of column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.  \n\n   *Disjointness.*  If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_i\\); if \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in C_j\\).  No cell belongs to both families, and no rectangle contains a diagonal cell.  \n\n   Thus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i\\}\\cup\\{C_j\\}\\) partition the whole board except the diagonal squares.  Relabelling the columns back to the original configuration yields a tiling for the original permutation with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\tau(n)=2n-2.\n   \\]  \n\n   For the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\tau(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns by \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); a unit square is identified with its pair \\((i,j)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row contains exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\\(f:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,f(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(f\\) is a bijection (a permutation).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to the diagonal  \n\nDefine a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) by \\(\\sigma(f(i))=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nRename column \\(j\\) as column \\(\\sigma(j)\\).  This relabelling does not change the set of unit squares covered by any tile, because a tile is a set of squares and the relabelling only changes the names of the columns.  Consequently the minimal number of tiles needed for the original configuration equals the minimal number needed after the relabelling.\n\nAfter the relabelling, (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal.  Hence it suffices to treat the case (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nLet a tiling of the complement \\(A=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U'\\) consist of \\(k\\) axis\u2011parallel rectangles.  \nFor a rectangle \\(T\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(T)=\\min\\{j\\mid\\exists i\\;(i,j)\\in T\\},\\qquad\nr(T)=\\max\\{j\\mid\\exists i\\;(i,j)\\in T\\}\n\\]\n\nits leftmost and rightmost column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma\u202f1 (rightmost columns)  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there exists a rectangle \\(T\\) with \\(r(T)=c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Because the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\), there is a unique row \\(i_0=c+1\\) whose uncovered square lies in column \\(c+1\\).  The cell \\((i_0,c)\\) is not uncovered, so it belongs to some rectangle \\(T\\).  Since column \\(c+1\\) is uncovered in row \\(i_0\\), \\(T\\) cannot contain column \\(c+1\\); therefore its column interval ends at column \\(c\\).  As \\(T\\) contains column \\(c\\), we have \\(r(T)=c\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma\u202f2 (leftmost columns)  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a rectangle \\(T\\) with \\(\\ell(T)=c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  There is a unique row \\(i_0=c-1\\) whose uncovered square lies in column \\(c-1\\).  The cell \\((i_0,c)\\) must be covered, so it lies in some rectangle \\(T\\).  Because column \\(c-1\\) is uncovered in row \\(i_0\\), \\(T\\) cannot contain column \\(c-1\\); hence its column interval starts at column \\(c\\).  Thus \\(\\ell(T)=c\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma\u202f3 (distinctness)  \nThe rectangles obtained in Lemma\u202f1 are all distinct, and the rectangles obtained in Lemma\u202f2 are all distinct.  Moreover no rectangle can appear in both families.\n\n*Proof.*  In Lemma\u202f1 each rectangle is identified by its rightmost column, which is different for different values of \\(c\\); hence the rectangles are distinct.  The same holds for Lemma\u202f2 with leftmost columns.  A rectangle cannot have rightmost column \\(c\\) and leftmost column \\(c'\\) with \\(c'<c\\); therefore a rectangle cannot satisfy the conclusion of both lemmas. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 we obtain at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles in any tiling of \\(A\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\ge 2n-2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nConsider the diagonal configuration (2).  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*   Each \\(R_i\\) and each \\(C_j\\) is a genuine rectangle: its set of rows and its set of columns are intervals of integers.  \n*   No rectangle contains a diagonal cell, because \\(R_i\\) uses only columns \\(>i\\) and \\(C_j\\) uses only rows \\(>j\\).  \n*   The families are disjoint: if \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_i\\); if \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in C_j\\); a cell cannot satisfy both inequalities, so it belongs to exactly one rectangle.  \n\nThus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{C_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) partition the whole set \\(A\\).  Hence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (3) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\); the construction of \u00a74 achieves this bound.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\tau(n)=2n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\tau(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
